// Handbook, Part 1: Foundations. See handbook-content.js for how chapters are structured.
// Checks without an `id` keep their original order, so saved progress still matches them.
(function () {
  const Fig = QT.hbFig;

  QT.hbChapters = Object.assign(QT.hbChapters || {}, {
    arith: {
      title: 'Numbers, fractions and fast arithmetic', minutes: 45,
      why: 'Arithmetic is the first filter at most trading firms, and it stays useful: every second you spend multiplying in an interview is a second you are not thinking about the problem.',
      goals: ['Convert between fractions, decimals and percentages instantly', 'Multiply two-digit numbers in your head with a handful of tricks', 'Divide, estimate square roots and compare fractions quickly', 'Check any answer by last digit, by size and by casting out nines', 'Handle compounding percentages and orders of magnitude'],
      sections: [
        { h: 'Why speed matters', blocks: [
          `<p>Most trading firms screen with a timed arithmetic test: Optiver's 80 questions in 8 minutes, Zetamac, Flow Traders' 10-minute test. Later, in interviews, quick arithmetic frees your attention for the actual problem.</p>
           <p>Speed doesn't come from calculating faster. It comes from <b>knowing a few facts cold</b>, so there's nothing to calculate, and from <b>a handful of tricks</b> that swap a hard sum for an easy one. This chapter gives you both, and the homework starts the daily habit that makes them automatic.</p>
           <p>A word on method. Tricks only pay if choosing them is instant, so practise <i>recognising</i> which one applies (are the numbers either side of a round number? near 100? ending in 5?) as much as doing the sums. And always check: a fast wrong answer costs more than a slightly slower right one, because tests usually penalise errors.</p>`,
        ] },
        { h: 'Fractions, decimals and percentages', blocks: [
          `<p>Memorise this table. These numbers come up constantly, and every other fraction is close to one of them.</p>
          <table class="hb-table"><tr><th>Fraction</th><th>Decimal</th><th>Fraction</th><th>Decimal</th></tr>
          <tr><td>1/2</td><td>0.5</td><td>1/7</td><td>0.142857…</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1/3</td><td>0.333…</td><td>1/8</td><td>0.125</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1/4</td><td>0.25</td><td>1/9</td><td>0.111…</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1/5</td><td>0.2</td><td>1/11</td><td>0.0909…</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1/6</td><td>0.1666…</td><td>1/12</td><td>0.08333…</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1/16</td><td>0.0625</td><td>1/32</td><td>0.03125</td></tr></table>
          <p>To convert anything else, <b>build it from these</b>: 3/8 = 3 × 0.125; 7/12 = 1/2 + 1/12; 5/6 = 1 − 1/6. Sevenths cycle through the digits 142857, starting at a different point for each numerator (2/7 = 0.285714…, 3/7 = 0.428571…).</p>
          <p>Ninths and elevenths have easy patterns too: n/9 = 0.nnn… (4/9 = 0.444…), and n/11 = 0.(9n)(9n)… repeating (3/11 = 0.2727…, 5/11 = 0.4545…). For an awkward fraction, find a nearby one you know: 7/15 is a little under 1/2 (7.5/15), precisely 0.4667.</p>`,
          { idea: 'A percentage is a fraction with 100 underneath, so x% of y equals y% of x. 8% of 25 looks awkward, but 25% of 8 is obviously 2.' },
          { q: 'Write 3/8 as a decimal.', a: 0.375, hint: 'It is three times 1/8.', sol: '1/8 = 0.125, so 3/8 = 3 × 0.125 = <b>0.375</b>. (Or 1/2 − 1/8 = 0.5 − 0.125.)' },
          { q: 'Write 7/12 as a decimal, to 3 decimal places.', a: 7 / 12, tol: { abs: 0.001 }, hint: 'Split it as 1/2 + 1/12.', sol: '7/12 = 6/12 + 1/12 = 1/2 + 1/12 = 0.5 + 0.0833 = <b>0.583</b>.' },
          { id: 'a1', q: 'What is 16% of 25?', a: 4, hint: 'Swap them: 25% of 16.', sol: 'x% of y = y% of x, so 16% of 25 = 25% of 16 = a quarter of 16 = <b>4</b>.' },
          `<p><b>Comparing fractions</b> without decimals: a/b > c/d exactly when ad > bc (cross-multiply, for positive numbers). Is 5/8 bigger than 7/11? 5 × 11 = 55 and 7 × 8 = 56, so 7/11 is (just) bigger. Multiple-choice tests love options that differ in the third decimal place, and cross-multiplying settles them exactly.</p>`,
        ] },
        { h: 'Multiplication is area', blocks: [
          `<p>Before the tricks, the one idea behind all of them. A product is the area of a rectangle, and you can cut a rectangle into pieces that are easy to add.</p>`,
          { fig: 'plot', x: [0, 26], y: [0, 19], w: 380, h: 260, alt: 'A 23 by 17 rectangle cut into four pieces: 20 by 10, 20 by 7, 3 by 10 and 3 by 7.', cap: '23 × 17 = 200 + 140 + 30 + 21 = 391. Every trick below is a clever way of cutting the rectangle.',
            polys: [{ pts: [[0, 0], [20, 0], [20, 10], [0, 10]], tone: 'acc-soft' }, { pts: [[20, 0], [23, 0], [23, 10], [20, 10]], tone: 'ok-soft' }, { pts: [[0, 10], [20, 10], [20, 17], [0, 17]], tone: 'ok-soft' }, { pts: [[20, 10], [23, 10], [23, 17], [20, 17]], tone: 'bad-soft' }],
            xt: [[20, '20'], [23, '23']], yt: [[10, '10'], [17, '17']], segs: [[20, 0, 20, 17, 'muted'], [0, 10, 23, 10, 'muted'], [23, 0, 23, 17, 'muted'], [0, 17, 23, 17, 'muted']],
            labels: [{ x: 10, y: 5, t: '20 × 10 = 200', a: 'middle' }, { x: 21.5, y: 5, t: '30', a: 'middle' }, { x: 10, y: 13.3, t: '20 × 7 = 140', a: 'middle' }, { x: 21.5, y: 13.3, t: '21', a: 'middle' }] },
          `<p>The general method is to split the larger number into tens and units and multiply each part: 23 × 17 = 20 × 17 + 3 × 17 = 340 + 51 = 391. Work left to right, keeping a running total, so you only ever hold one number in your head. That beats the written method, which works right to left and makes you remember the carries.</p>`,
        ] },
        { h: 'The tricks', blocks: [
          `<table class="hb-table"><tr><th>Trick</th><th>Rule</th><th>Example</th></tr>
            <tr><td>Split</td><td>a(b + c) = ab + ac</td><td>47 × 6 = 240 + 42 = 282</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Round and correct</td><td>a(100 − k) = 100a − ak</td><td>49 × 76 = 3800 − 76 = 3724</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Difference of squares</td><td>(n + k)(n − k) = n² − k²</td><td>38 × 42 = 1600 − 4 = 1596</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Near a base</td><td>(100 − a)(100 − b) = 100(100 − a − b) + ab</td><td>97 × 96 = 9300 + 12 = 9312</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Ends in 5</td><td>(10a + 5)² = a(a + 1) × 100 + 25</td><td>65² = 42 then 25 = 4225</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Times 25</td><td>× 100 then ÷ 4</td><td>48 × 25 = 1200</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Times 11</td><td>add neighbouring digits</td><td>63 × 11: 6, 6 + 3, 3 = 693</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Squares near 50</td><td>(50 + k)² = 2500 + 100k + k²</td><td>53² = 2500 + 300 + 9 = 2809</td></tr></table>
            <p>Always finish with two checks. The <b>last digit</b>: 7 × 6 ends in 2, so 97 × 96 must end in 2. The <b>size</b>: 97 × 96 must be a little under 100 × 100. These take a second and catch nearly every slip.</p>
            <p>Why near-base works: (100 − a)(100 − b) = 10000 − 100a − 100b + ab = 100(100 − a − b) + ab. The same holds around any base, such as 50 or 1000, and for numbers just above the base, where the gaps are negative: 103 × 104 = 100 × 107 + 12 = 10,712.</p>`,
          { worked: 'near a base', q: 'Work out 93 × 96 in your head.', steps: [
            ['How far is each number from 100?', '7 and 4.'],
            ['Subtract the gaps from 100.', '100 − 7 − 4 = 89, which gives 8900.'],
            ['Now the correction: what do you add?', 'The product of the gaps: 7 × 4 = 28. Total 8928.'],
            ['Check it.', 'Last digit: 3 × 6 = 18 ends in 8 ✓. Size: a bit under 9000 ✓.'],
          ] },
          { q: '47 × 53', a: 2491, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: 'They sit either side of 50.', sol: '47 and 53 are 50 ∓ 3, so the product is 50² − 3² = 2500 − 9 = <b>2491</b>. Check: last digit 7 × 3 = 21 → 1 ✓.' },
          { q: '98 × 97', a: 9506, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: 'Both are near 100: subtract both gaps from 100, then add the product of the gaps.', sol: 'Gaps from 100: 2 and 3. 100 − 2 − 3 = 95, giving 9500; add 2 × 3 = 6: <b>9506</b>. Check: 8 × 7 = 56 → 6 ✓, and a bit under 10,000 ✓.' },
          { id: 'a2', q: '85²', a: 7225, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: 'Ends in 5: 8 × 9, then 25.', sol: 'For a number ending in 5, multiply the tens digit by one more than itself and write 25 after it: 8 × 9 = 72, then 25: <b>7225</b>. Why: (10a + 5)² = 100a² + 100a + 25 = 100a(a + 1) + 25.' },
          { id: 'm1', q: 'Someone says 87 × 43 = 3742. Without multiplying, what is wrong?', opts: [['Its last digit should be 1'], ['It is too small, as 87 × 43 is over 4000', 'Compare with 90 × 40 = 3600: the size looks fine.'], ['It should be even, as one factor is even', 'Both 87 and 43 are odd.'], ['Nothing, it passes both checks', 'Try the last-digit check.']], sol: '7 × 3 = 21 ends in 1, so the true answer must too. It is 3741.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Division, square roots and a third check', blocks: [
          `<p><b>Division</b> is multiplication backwards, so use the same facts. Dividing by 5 is doubling then dividing by 10 (135/5 = 270/10 = 27); by 25 is multiplying by 4 then dividing by 100; by 125 is multiplying by 8 then dividing by 1000. For anything else, estimate first: 1,000/7 is about 143, because 1/7 ≈ 0.1429.</p>
           <p><b>Square roots</b> by one step of Newton's method: if a² is a square close to x, then √x ≈ a + (x − a²)/(2a). √50 ≈ 7 + 1/14 ≈ 7.071 (true 7.0711). √80 ≈ 9 − 1/18 ≈ 8.944 (true 8.9443). It works because the square-root curve is nearly straight over a short distance.</p>
           <p><b>Casting out nines</b> catches errors the last-digit check misses. A number leaves the same remainder on division by 9 as the sum of its digits. So check a product by multiplying the digit sums: 87 × 43: digit sums 15 → 6 and 7; 6 × 7 = 42 → 6. The answer 3741 has digit sum 15 → 6 ✓. A transposed answer such as 3714 also passes, so this check misses swaps of digits, but it catches most dropped or added amounts.</p>`,
          { id: 'x1', q: 'Estimate √50 to two decimal places, without a calculator.', a: Math.sqrt(50), tol: { abs: 0.01 }, hint: '7² = 49. Use √x ≈ a + (x − a²)/(2a).', sol: '√50 ≈ 7 + (50 − 49)/14 = 7 + 0.0714 ≈ <b>7.07</b>.' },
          { id: 'x2', q: 'Which is larger: 7/9 or 11/14? Give the larger one as a decimal (3 places).', a: 11 / 14, tol: { abs: 0.001 }, hint: 'Cross-multiply: compare 7 × 14 with 11 × 9.', sol: '7 × 14 = 98 and 11 × 9 = 99, so 11/14 is larger, by a hair: 11/14 ≈ <b>0.786</b> (7/9 ≈ 0.778).' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Percentages compound', blocks: [
          `<p>Percentage changes multiply rather than add. Up 20% then down 20% leaves you at 1.2 × 0.8 = 0.96: down 4%. The base changed between the two moves.</p>`,
          { fig: 'bars', vals: [100, 120, 96], labs: ['Start', 'Up 20%', 'Then down 20%'], show: ['100', '120', '96'], tone: (i) => (i === 2 ? 'bad' : ''), h: 190, w: 320, alt: 'Bars at 100, 120 and 96, showing that up 20 percent then down 20 percent ends below the start.', cap: 'A 20% fall from 120 is 24, bigger than the 20 you gained.' },
          `<p>For small changes, (1 + x)(1 + y) ≈ 1 + x + y, and the xy term is the error in just adding them. A related shortcut, the <b>rule of 72</b>: at r% a period, money doubles in about 72/r periods. It works because ln 2 ≈ 0.69 and compounding nudges it up.</p>
           <p>Percentage points versus percent is the other classic trap. A rate rising from 4% to 5% has risen by 1 percentage point, but by 25% of itself. Trading desks avoid the ambiguity by quoting changes in rates and spreads in basis points.</p>`,
          { trap: 'Losses need bigger gains to recover. After a 50% loss you need +100% to get back, which is why risk limits matter so much more than they first appear to.' },
          { q: 'A price rises 10%, then another 10%. By what factor has it grown?', a: 1.21, hint: 'Multiply the two factors.', sol: 'Each rise multiplies by 1.1, so the total factor is 1.1 × 1.1 = <b>1.21</b>: up 21%, not 20%. The extra 1% is 10% of the first 10% gain.' },
          { worked: 'reversing a percentage', q: 'A jacket costs £96 after a 20% discount. What was the original price?', steps: [
            ['What fraction of the original is £96?', '80%, i.e. 0.8 of it.'],
            ['So the original is?', '96/0.8 = £120.'],
            'The trap is adding 20% to £96 (giving £115.20): the 20% was taken off the original, not the sale price.',
          ] },
        ] },
        { h: 'Orders of magnitude', blocks: [
          `<p>Know 2<sup>10</sup> = 1024 ≈ 10³, log₁₀ 2 ≈ 0.301, log₁₀ 3 ≈ 0.477 and ln 10 ≈ 2.303. They turn big products into small sums: 2<sup>20</sup> ≈ 10⁶, and 3<sup>10</sup> = 10<sup>4.77</sup> ≈ 59,000. The number of digits of N is ⌊log₁₀ N⌋ + 1.</p>
           <p>From log₁₀ 2 and log₁₀ 3 you get many others for free: log₁₀ 4 = 0.602, log₁₀ 5 = 1 − 0.301 = 0.699, log₁₀ 6 = 0.778, log₁₀ 8 = 0.903, log₁₀ 9 = 0.954. So 5<sup>10</sup> = 10<sup>6.99</sup> ≈ 9.8 million.</p>`,
          { market: 'Traders think in basis points. One bp is 0.01%, so 100 bps = 1%. A 3 bp cost on a $10m trade is $3,000: move the decimal rather than reach for a calculator.' },
          { q: 'How many digits does 2<sup>20</sup> have?', a: 7, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: 'Use 2¹⁰ = 1024 and square it, or log₁₀ 2 ≈ 0.301.', sol: '2²⁰ = 1024² = 1,048,576: <b>7</b> digits. Or 20 × 0.301 = 6.02, so ⌊6.02⌋ + 1 = 7.' },
        ] },
      ],
      takeaways: ['Know the common fractions as decimals by heart; build others from them. Compare fractions by cross-multiplying.', 'x% of y = y% of x.', 'Round-and-correct, difference of squares and near-base cover most multiplications.', 'Divide by 5, 25, 125 by multiplying by 2, 4, 8. √x ≈ a + (x − a²)/(2a).', 'Always check the last digit and the size; casting out nines catches more.', 'Percentages multiply; percentage points are not percent; 2¹⁰ ≈ 10³ and log₁₀ 2 ≈ 0.3 handle magnitudes.'],
      explain: { q: 'Why does 38 × 42 equal 40² − 4, and when is this trick available?', a: 'Multiply out (40 − 2)(40 + 2): the cross terms +80 and −80 cancel, leaving 1600 − 4. It works whenever the two numbers are the same distance either side of a number you can square easily, so their average must be round. Their sum must be even, so 37 × 42 doesn\'t qualify directly; do 37 × 42 = 38 × 42 − 42 instead.' },
      homework: [
        { lvl: 'core', q: '73 × 67', a: 4891, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: 'They sit either side of 70.', sol: '<p><b>Trick:</b> difference of squares, since 73 and 67 are 70 ± 3.</p><ol><li>70² = 4900.</li><li>3² = 9.</li><li>4900 − 9 = <b>4891</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Check:</b> last digit 3 × 7 = 21 → 1 ✓; size near 70 × 70 ✓.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: '99 × 87', a: 8613, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: 'Round and correct.', sol: '<p><b>Trick:</b> round 99 up to 100, then take back one 87.</p><ol><li>100 × 87 = 8700.</li><li>8700 − 87 = <b>8613</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Check:</b> 9 × 7 = 63 → 3 ✓. The near-base method agrees: gaps 1 and 13, 100 − 14 = 86 → 8600, plus 13 = 8613.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'Write 5/8 as a decimal.', a: 0.625, hint: 'Five eighths.', sol: '<p>1/8 = 0.125, so 5/8 = 5 × 0.125 = <b>0.625</b>. Or: 5/8 = 1/2 + 1/8 = 0.5 + 0.125.</p><p>All the eighths are worth knowing: 0.125, 0.25, 0.375, 0.5, 0.625, 0.75, 0.875.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: '35²', a: 1225, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: '3 × 4, then 25.', sol: '<p><b>Trick:</b> ends in 5, so take the tens digit times one more than itself, then write 25.</p><ol><li>3 × 4 = 12.</li><li>Append 25: <b>1225</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Why it works:</b> 35² = (30 + 5)² = 900 + 300 + 25 = 1225, and 900 + 300 = 30 × 40 = 1200.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'A stock falls 25%. As a fraction, what rise takes it back to where it started?', a: 1 / 3, tol: { abs: 0.005 }, hint: 'Start at 100. It falls to 75. What must 75 gain?', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> work with a concrete price.</p><ol><li>Start at 100; after a 25% fall it is 75.</li><li>To get back to 100 it must gain 25, which is 25/75 = <b>1/3</b> of its new price, about 33%.</li></ol><p><b>General rule:</b> after a fall of f, the recovery needed is f/(1 − f). A 10% fall needs 11%; 50% needs 100%; 90% needs 900%.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'How many digits does 3<sup>20</sup> have? No calculator.', a: 10, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: 'log₁₀ 3 ≈ 0.477.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> digits = ⌊log₁₀ N⌋ + 1.</p><ol><li>log₁₀ 3²⁰ = 20 × 0.477 = 9.54.</li><li>⌊9.54⌋ + 1 = <b>10</b> digits.</li></ol><p><b>Check:</b> 3¹⁰ = 59,049 ≈ 6 × 10⁴, so 3²⁰ ≈ 36 × 10⁸ = 3.6 × 10⁹: ten digits. (Exactly 3,486,784,401.)</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'You pay 8 bps on a $25m trade. What is the cost in dollars?', a: 20000, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: '1 bp of $25m is $2,500.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> 1 bp is 1/10,000, so find one basis point first.</p><ol><li>$25,000,000/10,000 = $2,500 per bp.</li><li>8 × $2,500 = <b>$20,000</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Habit:</b> 1 bp of $1m is $100. Scale from there: $25m makes $2,500 a bp.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'stretch', q: 'Estimate 1.02<sup>35</sup> to one decimal place using the rule of 72.', a: 1.02 ** 35, tol: { abs: 0.1 }, hint: 'How long does 2% take to double?', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> rule of 72.</p><ol><li>At 2% a period, money doubles in about 72/2 = 36 periods.</li><li>35 periods is just short of that, so 1.02³⁵ ≈ <b>2.0</b>.</li></ol><p><b>A finer check:</b> 1.02³⁵ = e<sup>35 ln 1.02</sup> ≈ e<sup>35 × 0.0198</sup> = e<sup>0.693</sup> ≈ 2.00. (Exactly 1.9999.) Why 72 rather than 69: ln 2 = 0.693 gives 69.3/r for continuous growth, and 72 is close, a bit better for typical rates, and divisible by 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 9 and 12.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'lab', q: 'Start the habit: play one two-minute Zetamac game a day this week and write down each score. Which operation slowed you down most?', sol: '<p>Most people lose time on subtraction with borrowing and two-digit multiplication. Open Mental maths in the app, and use Flow training to find your own slowest kinds.</p><p><b>What improvement looks like:</b> scores usually climb fast for the first week or two as the tricks become automatic, then slowly. Daily short sessions beat occasional long ones, because the goal is recall without thinking.</p>' },
      ],
      practice: [['Speed tricks', '#/tricks'], ['Zetamac', '#/mental'], ['Flow training', '#/mental/flow']],
    },

    toolkit: {
      title: 'The maths toolkit', minutes: 50,
      why: 'Later chapters need a little algebra and calculus: logs for returns, geometric series for repeated games, derivatives for optimal bets, and matrices for portfolios. If it is fresh, skim this. If it is rusty, this is the chapter that stops you getting stuck later.',
      goals: ['Use exponentials and logs fluently, including log returns', 'Sum arithmetic and geometric series', 'Approximate with Taylor series and the limit that defines e', 'Maximise a function with a derivative', 'Integrate simple functions and read an integral as an area', 'Multiply a matrix by a vector and write a quadratic form'],
      prereq: ['arith'],
      sections: [
        { h: 'Exponentials and logs', blocks: [
          `<p>A logarithm undoes an exponential: ln(e<sup>x</sup>) = x. The rule that matters is that logs turn products into sums:</p>
           <div class="hb-eq">ln(ab) = ln a + ln b &nbsp;&nbsp; ln(a<sup>k</sup>) = k ln a &nbsp;&nbsp; ln(1 + x) ≈ x for small x</div>
           <p>That's why traders use <b>log returns</b>. If a price goes from P₀ to P₁ to P₂, the simple returns compound by multiplying, but the log returns just add: ln(P₂/P₀) = ln(P₂/P₁) + ln(P₁/P₀).</p>
           <p>Two more facts make logs usable in your head. Changing base is a division: log<sub>b</sub> x = ln x/ln b, so log₂ 1000 = ln 1000/ln 2 ≈ 6.91/0.693 ≈ 9.97. And for small moves a log return and a simple return are almost the same (ln 1.01 ≈ 0.00995), with the gap growing like x²/2. For a daily move of 1% that gap is irrelevant; for a 50% move it's huge (ln 1.5 ≈ 0.405).</p>
           <p>Log returns are also symmetric where simple returns aren't: +10% then −10% loses money, but a log return of +0.1 followed by −0.1 brings you exactly back. That's why models of prices work with log returns.</p>`,
          { fig: 'plot', x: [-2, 4], y: [-2, 5], xt: [-2, 0, 2, 4], yt: [-2, 0, 2, 4], fns: [{ f: Math.exp, tone: 'acc' }, { f: Math.log, from: 0.05, tone: 'ok' }, { f: (x) => x, tone: 'muted', dash: true }], labels: [{ x: 1.6, y: 4.5, t: 'eˣ', tone: 'acc' }, { x: 3, y: 0.6, t: 'ln x', tone: 'ok' }, { x: 3.4, y: 3.9, t: 'y = x' }], alt: 'The curves e to the x and natural log of x, mirror images in the line y equals x.', cap: 'ln and e<sup>x</sup> are mirror images in the line y = x, because each undoes the other.' },
          { q: 'What is ln(e³ × e⁴)?', a: 7, hint: 'Exponents add, or logs add.', sol: 'e³ × e⁴ = e⁷ (exponents add), and ln undoes e: ln(e⁷) = <b>7</b>. Or with logs: ln e³ + ln e⁴ = 3 + 4.' },
          { q: 'A stock returns +10% then −10% in simple terms. What is the sum of its log returns, to 3 decimal places?', a: Math.log(1.1) + Math.log(0.9), tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'ln(1.1 × 0.9) = ln(0.99).', sol: 'Log returns add: ln 1.1 + ln 0.9 = ln(1.1 × 0.9) = ln 0.99 ≈ <b>−0.010</b>. Negative, even though the simple returns seem to cancel: you really did lose 1% (110 × 0.9 = 99).' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Series you need', blocks: [
          `<p>Two sums appear everywhere.</p>
           <div class="hb-eq">1 + 2 + … + n = n(n + 1)/2<br>1 + r + r² + … = 1/(1 − r), for |r| &lt; 1</div>
           <p>The geometric series is what you get whenever a game can repeat with probability r. The picture proves the case r = ½: keep halving what's left of a unit square, and you fill it.</p>
           <p>Two variants come up constantly. A <b>finite</b> geometric sum: 1 + r + … + r<sup>n−1</sup> = (1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r) (the infinite sum minus its tail, which is rⁿ times the whole). And a <b>first term other than 1</b>: a + ar + ar² + … = a/(1 − r). In words: first term over one minus the ratio.</p>`,
          { fig: 'plot', x: [0, 1], y: [0, 1], w: 240, h: 240, polys: [{ pts: [[0, 0], [0.5, 0], [0.5, 1], [0, 1]], tone: 'acc-soft' }, { pts: [[0.5, 0], [1, 0], [1, 0.5], [0.5, 0.5]], tone: 'ok-soft' }, { pts: [[0.5, 0.5], [0.75, 0.5], [0.75, 1], [0.5, 1]], tone: 'bad-soft' }, { pts: [[0.75, 0.5], [1, 0.5], [1, 0.75], [0.75, 0.75]], tone: 'acc-soft' }, { pts: [[0.75, 0.75], [0.875, 0.75], [0.875, 1], [0.75, 1]], tone: 'ok-soft' }], labels: [{ x: 0.25, y: 0.48, t: '½', a: 'middle' }, { x: 0.75, y: 0.22, t: '¼', a: 'middle' }, { x: 0.625, y: 0.72, t: '⅛', a: 'middle' }], alt: 'A unit square cut into a half, a quarter, an eighth and so on.', cap: '½ + ¼ + ⅛ + … = 1.' },
          { proof: 'Let S = 1 + r + r² + …. Then rS = r + r² + …, so S − rS = 1 and S = 1/(1 − r). The series only makes sense when |r| &lt; 1, since otherwise the terms don\'t shrink.' },
          { worked: true, q: 'You and a friend take turns to flip a coin, you first. The first head wins. What is your chance of winning?', steps: [
            ['In which rounds can you win?', 'On flip 1, 3, 5, and so on.'],
            ['What is the chance you win on each?', '½ on flip 1, then (½)³ on flip 3, because two tails must come first, then (½)⁵, and so on.'],
            ['This is geometric: what are the first term and ratio?', 'First term ½, ratio ¼.'],
            'The total is (½)/(1 − ¼) = 2/3.',
          ] },
          { q: 'What is 1 + 2 + … + 100?', a: 5050, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: 'n(n + 1)/2.', sol: 'Pair the terms from the ends: 1 + 100, 2 + 99, …, 50 + 51 makes 50 pairs of 101. So 100 × 101/2 = <b>5050</b>.' },
          { q: 'What is 1 + ⅓ + (⅓)² + …?', a: 1.5, hint: '1/(1 − r).', sol: 'Geometric with first term 1 and ratio ⅓: 1/(1 − ⅓) = 1/(2/3) = <b>1.5</b>.' },
          { opts: [['The series has no finite sum'], ['It equals 1/(1 − 1.1) = −10', 'A sum of positive terms can\'t be negative. The formula doesn\'t apply here.'], ['It equals 11', 'The terms grow, so the sum never settles.'], ['Series can\'t have a ratio above 1', 'They can, but then they diverge.']], q: 'A friend says 1 + 1.1 + 1.1² + … = −10. What went wrong?', sol: 'The formula is only valid when the terms shrink. Here they grow without limit, so the sum is infinite.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Approximations: Taylor series and e', blocks: [
          `<p>Near a point, a smooth function looks like a polynomial. Around 0:</p>
           <div class="hb-eq">eˣ ≈ 1 + x + x²/2 &nbsp;&nbsp; ln(1 + x) ≈ x − x²/2 &nbsp;&nbsp; (1 + x)ⁿ ≈ 1 + nx &nbsp;&nbsp; √(1 + x) ≈ 1 + x/2</div>
           <p>Keep the first term for a rough answer, the second for a good one. 1.03¹⁰ ≈ 1 + 0.3 = 1.30 (true 1.344; the next term, C(10, 2) × 0.03² = 0.04, closes the gap). √1.1 ≈ 1.05 (true 1.0488). These are how you estimate compound growth and volatility in your head.</p>
           <p>The most important limit in finance comes from the same place:</p>
           <div class="hb-eq">(1 + x/n)ⁿ → eˣ as n → ∞</div>
           <p>It is compounding: 100% interest paid in n instalments grows £1 to (1 + 1/n)ⁿ, which rises to e ≈ 2.718 as you compound more often. Its twin, (1 − 1/n)ⁿ → 1/e ≈ 0.368, is the chance of missing a 1-in-n event in n tries, which you'll meet in probability again and again.</p>`,
          { id: 'x1', q: 'Estimate 0.99¹⁰⁰ using (1 + x/n)ⁿ ≈ eˣ. Give a decimal.', a: Math.exp(-1), tol: { abs: 0.003 }, hint: '0.99 = 1 − 1/100.', sol: '0.99¹⁰⁰ = (1 − 1/100)¹⁰⁰ ≈ e<sup>−1</sup> ≈ <b>0.368</b>. (The exact value is 0.366.)' },
          { worked: 'how much does daily compounding add?', q: 'A 5% annual rate is compounded daily (365 times a year). How much more than simple annual interest does it pay on £1,000?', steps: [
            ['What does daily compounding grow £1 to?', '(1 + 0.05/365)³⁶⁵ ≈ e<sup>0.05</sup>.'],
            ['Approximate e<sup>0.05</sup>.', '1 + 0.05 + 0.05²/2 = 1.05125.'],
            'So £1,000 becomes about £1,051.25 against £1,050: about <b>£1.25</b> more. The x²/2 term is the whole difference.',
          ] },
        ] },
        { h: 'Derivatives find the best choice', blocks: [
          `<p>The derivative f′(x) is the slope of f at x. At the top of a smooth hill the slope is zero, so to maximise f, solve f′(x) = 0 and check it's a maximum (f″ &lt; 0). You'll use this for the Kelly bet, least squares and maximum likelihood.</p>
           <div class="hb-eq">(xⁿ)′ = n xⁿ⁻¹ &nbsp;&nbsp; (eˣ)′ = eˣ &nbsp;&nbsp; (ln x)′ = 1/x &nbsp;&nbsp; (f(g(x)))′ = f′(g(x)) g′(x)</div>`,
          { fig: 'plot', x: [0, 1], y: [0, 0.3], xt: [0, 0.5, 1], yt: [0, 0.1, 0.2], xl: 'x', fns: [{ f: (x) => x * (1 - x) }], segs: [[0.3, 0.25, 0.7, 0.25, 'ok']], pts: [{ x: 0.5, y: 0.25, tone: 'ok' }], labels: [{ x: 0.52, y: 0.27, t: 'slope is zero at the top', tone: 'ok' }], alt: 'The curve x times 1 minus x, with a flat tangent at its peak at x equals one half.', cap: 'f(x) = x(1 − x) has f′(x) = 1 − 2x, which is zero at x = ½. That\'s why a coin with p = ½ has the largest variance.' },
          { q: 'Where does f(x) = 6x − x² reach its maximum?', a: 3, hint: 'Set f′(x) = 6 − 2x to zero.', sol: 'f′(x) = 6 − 2x, which is 0 at x = <b>3</b>. f″(x) = −2 < 0, so it is a maximum, with f(3) = 9. (Completing the square agrees: 6x − x² = 9 − (x − 3)².)' },
          { q: 'What is the derivative of ln(1 + 2x) at x = 0?', a: 2, hint: 'Chain rule: 2/(1 + 2x).', sol: 'Chain rule: the outside function ln(u) has derivative 1/u, and the inside u = 1 + 2x has derivative 2. So the derivative is 2/(1 + 2x), which is 2/(1 + 0) = <b>2</b> at x = 0. That matches ln(1 + 2x) ≈ 2x for small x.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Integrals are areas', blocks: [
          `<p>∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx is the area under f between a and b, and undoes differentiation: ∫ xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1). For a continuous random variable, probabilities are areas under its density, so this is how we'll compute them.</p>
           <p>Other integrals you'll need: ∫ eᵃˣ dx = eᵃˣ/a; ∫ 1/x dx = ln x; and ∫₀^∞ e<sup>−λx</sup> dx = 1/λ, which is why an exponential waiting time with rate λ has density λe<sup>−λx</sup> (it must integrate to 1). For a product such as x e<sup>−x</sup>, use <b>integration by parts</b>, ∫u v′ = uv − ∫u′v: ∫₀^∞ x e<sup>−x</sup> dx = [−x e<sup>−x</sup>]₀^∞ + ∫₀^∞ e<sup>−x</sup> dx = 0 + 1 = 1. That is the mean of an exponential with rate 1.</p>`,
          { fig: 'plot', x: [0, 1.1], y: [0, 1.2], xt: [0, 1], yt: [0, 1], fns: [{ f: (x) => x * x }], area: [{ f: (x) => x * x, from: 0, to: 1 }], labels: [{ x: 0.73, y: 0.16, t: 'area = 1/3', a: 'middle' }], alt: 'The area under x squared from 0 to 1, which equals one third.', cap: '∫₀¹ x² dx = [x³/3]₀¹ = 1/3.' },
          { q: 'What is ∫₀² 3x² dx?', a: 8, hint: 'The antiderivative is x³.', sol: 'An antiderivative of 3x² is x³ (differentiate to check). Evaluate between the limits: 2³ − 0³ = <b>8</b>.' },
          { id: 'x2', q: 'What is ∫₀^∞ x e<sup>−2x</sup> dx? (The mean of an exponential waiting time with rate 2.)', a: 0.25, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'By parts, or: the mean of an exponential with rate λ is 1/λ, and this integral is that mean divided by λ.', sol: 'By parts: ∫₀^∞ x e<sup>−2x</sup> dx = [−x e<sup>−2x</sup>/2]₀^∞ + ½∫₀^∞ e<sup>−2x</sup> dx = 0 + ½ × ½ = <b>0.25</b>. (The density is 2e<sup>−2x</sup>, so the mean is 2 × 0.25 = 0.5 = 1/λ.)' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Vectors and matrices', blocks: [
          `<p>A vector is a list of numbers: a portfolio's weights w, or its stocks' returns r. The dot product w·r = Σ wᵢrᵢ is the portfolio's return. A matrix is a table of numbers that turns a vector into another vector: each output entry is the dot product of one row with the input.</p>
           <div class="hb-eq">[1 2; 3 4] × [5, 6] = [1×5 + 2×6, 3×5 + 4×6] = [17, 39]</div>
           <p>The form you'll use most is the <b>quadratic form</b> wᵀΣw, which is the variance of a portfolio with covariance matrix Σ. Work it out once for two assets and it stops being mysterious:</p>
           <div class="hb-eq">[w₁ w₂] [σ₁² c; c σ₂²] [w₁, w₂] = w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2w₁w₂c</div>
           <p>An <b>eigenvector</b> v of a matrix A is a direction that A only stretches: Av = λv, where the number λ is its eigenvalue. The next chapter goes further, and the correlation matrices chapter uses this to find the main sources of risk in a market.</p>`,
          { q: 'What is the dot product of [0.5, 0.5] and [0.04, 0.02]?', a: 0.03, hint: 'Multiply pairs, then add.', sol: 'Multiply matching entries and add: 0.5 × 0.04 + 0.5 × 0.02 = 0.02 + 0.01 = <b>0.03</b>: half the money made 4% and half made 2%, so the portfolio returned 3%.' },
          { q: 'Weights [1, 1] and covariance matrix [4 1; 1 9]. What is wᵀΣw?', a: 15, hint: 'Add up every entry of the matrix.', sol: 'With weights all 1, wᵀΣw is the sum of every entry of Σ: 4 + 1 + 1 + 9 = <b>15</b>. In variance terms: Var(X + Y) = Var X + Var Y + 2Cov = 4 + 9 + 2.' },
          { opts: [['2, as Av = 2v'], ['[2, 2]: the eigenvalue is the output', 'That\'s the output vector. The eigenvalue is the number multiplying v.'], ['1: the entries of v are 1', 'Compare Av with v.'], ['4: the sum of the output', 'Compare Av with v.']], q: 'A = [2 0; 0 2] and v = [1, 1]. What is the eigenvalue for v?', sol: 'Av = [2 × 1 + 0 × 1, 0 × 1 + 2 × 1] = [2, 2] = 2v, so λ = 2. This A doubles every vector, so every direction is an eigenvector with eigenvalue 2.' },
        ] },
      ],
      takeaways: ['Logs turn products into sums, so log returns add.', 'n(n + 1)/2 and 1/(1 − r) cover most sums; finite geometric sums are (1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r).', 'eˣ ≈ 1 + x + x²/2 and (1 + x/n)ⁿ → eˣ: compounding and "1 − 1/e".', 'Maximise by setting the derivative to zero.', 'Integrals are areas, and ∫xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1).', 'A portfolio\'s return is w·r and its variance is wᵀΣw.'],
      explain: { q: 'Why do traders add log returns, but have to multiply simple returns?', a: 'Wealth grows by multiplying (1 + return) factors. Taking logs turns that product into a sum, so log returns over consecutive periods simply add, which also makes their averages and variances easier to work with.' },
      homework: [
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'A price goes from 100 to 130. To 3 decimal places, what is the log return?', a: Math.log(1.3), tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'ln(130/100).', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> log return = ln(new/old).</p><ol><li>ln(130/100) = ln 1.3.</li><li>Estimate: ln(1 + x) ≈ x − x²/2 + x³/3 = 0.3 − 0.045 + 0.009 ≈ 0.264. The exact value is ≈ <b>0.262</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Point:</b> the log return is smaller than the simple return of 0.30, and the gap grows with the size of the move. For small daily moves the two are almost identical.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'What is 2 + 4 + 6 + … + 200?', a: 10100, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: 'Take out a factor of 2.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> factor out the 2 to reach a sum you know.</p><ol><li>2 + 4 + … + 200 = 2 × (1 + 2 + … + 100).</li><li>1 + … + 100 = 100 × 101/2 = 5050.</li><li>2 × 5050 = <b>10,100</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Check with pairing:</b> 100 terms, pairing first and last gives 50 pairs of 202 = 10,100.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'A game pays $1 now, and then continues with probability 0.9, paying $1 each round. What is its expected total?', a: 10, hint: '1 + 0.9 + 0.9² + …', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> add the expected payment in each round.</p><ol><li>Round 1 is certain: $1. Round 2 happens with probability 0.9, round 3 with 0.9², and so on.</li><li>Expected total = 1 + 0.9 + 0.9² + … = 1/(1 − 0.9) = <b>$10</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Another view:</b> the number of rounds is geometric with "stop" probability 0.1, so it averages 1/0.1 = 10 rounds at $1 each.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'Find the x that maximises f(x) = −x ln x on (0, 1). Give it to 3 decimal places.', a: 1 / Math.E, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'f′(x) = −ln x − 1.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> set the derivative to zero.</p><ol><li>Product rule: f′(x) = −(1 × ln x + x × 1/x) = −ln x − 1.</li><li>f′(x) = 0 ⇒ ln x = −1 ⇒ x = 1/e ≈ <b>0.368</b>.</li><li>f″(x) = −1/x < 0, so it is a maximum, with value 1/e too.</li></ol><p>You\'ll meet 1/e again in the secretary problem. (−x ln x is also the building block of entropy.)</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'What is ∫₀¹ (x + x²) dx? Give a fraction or decimal.', a: 5 / 6, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'Integrate term by term.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> integrals of sums split term by term.</p><ol><li>∫₀¹ x dx = [x²/2]₀¹ = ½.</li><li>∫₀¹ x² dx = [x³/3]₀¹ = ⅓.</li><li>½ + ⅓ = <b>5/6</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Sense check:</b> on [0, 1] the function runs from 0 to 2, so an area a bit under 1 is right.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'Two assets have variances 0.04 and 0.09 and covariance 0.01. Weights are 0.5 each. What is the portfolio variance?', a: 0.0375, hint: 'w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2w₁w₂c.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> the quadratic form wᵀΣw written out for two assets.</p><ol><li>w₁²σ₁² = 0.25 × 0.04 = 0.01.</li><li>w₂²σ₂² = 0.25 × 0.09 = 0.0225.</li><li>2w₁w₂c = 2 × 0.25 × 0.01 = 0.005.</li><li>Total: <b>0.0375</b>, a volatility of √0.0375 ≈ 19.4%.</li></ol><p><b>Check:</b> the two volatilities are 20% and 30%; a 50/50 mix with low correlation (c/(σ₁σ₂) = 0.01/0.06 ≈ 0.17) should be below their average of 25%. It is.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'stretch', q: 'Prove that 1 + 2r + 3r² + 4r³ + … = 1/(1 − r)² for |r| &lt; 1. Use it to find the mean of a geometric distribution with p = ½.', sol: '<p><b>Proof:</b> differentiate the geometric series term by term (allowed for |r| &lt; 1).</p><ol><li>1 + r + r² + r³ + … = 1/(1 − r).</li><li>Differentiate both sides in r: 1 + 2r + 3r² + … = 1/(1 − r)².</li></ol><p><b>Geometric mean:</b> if each try succeeds with probability p, the first success comes on try k with probability p(1 − p)<sup>k−1</sup>. So the mean is Σ k p(1 − p)<sup>k−1</sup> = p × 1/(1 − (1 − p))² = p/p² = 1/p, which is <b>2</b> when p = ½.</p><p>Differentiating a known series is a standard trick for sums with a k in front.</p>' },
      ],
      practice: [['Formula cards', '#/flashcards'], ['Returns, risk and sizing', '#/topic/finance']],
    },

    counting: {
      title: 'Counting', minutes: 45,
      why: 'Half of probability is counting: how many outcomes are there, and how many do you want? Counting well is mostly a matter of asking whether order matters and whether things can repeat.',
      goals: ['Count arrangements with the multiplication rule, including restrictions', 'Know when order matters: permutations v combinations', 'Use repeated items, lattice paths and stars and bars', 'Count with complements and inclusion–exclusion, including derangements', 'Count poker hands without getting lost'],
      prereq: ['arith'],
      sections: [
        { h: 'The multiplication rule', blocks: [
          `<p>If a choice happens in stages, with a ways for the first stage and b for the second (whatever the first was), there are a × b outcomes in total. A 3-letter code has 26 × 26 × 26 possibilities; a 3-letter code with <i>no repeated letter</i> has 26 × 25 × 24.</p>
           <p>The phrase "whatever the first was" is the whole rule. The <i>number</i> of options at each stage must not depend on earlier choices, even if the options themselves do. When it does depend, either reorder the stages so the awkward one comes first, or split into cases and add.</p>`,
          { fig: 'tree', root: { kids: [{ t: 'A', kids: [{ t: 'AA' }, { t: 'AB' }, { t: 'AC' }] }, { t: 'B', kids: [{ t: 'BA' }, { t: 'BB' }, { t: 'BC' }] }] }, colW: 100, rowH: 26, leafW: 60, alt: 'A tree with two first choices, each leading to three second choices, for six leaves.', cap: 'Two first choices times three second choices: six paths. Every leaf is one outcome.' },
          { widget: 'counting' },
          { q: 'How many 3-letter codes can be made from 26 letters if letters may repeat?', a: 17576, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: 'Three stages, 26 choices each.', sol: 'Three independent stages with 26 choices each: 26 × 26 × 26 = 26³ = <b>17,576</b>.' },
          { worked: 'put the restricted stage first', q: 'How many 3-digit numbers (100 to 999) have all digits different?', steps: [
            ['Which digit has a special rule?', 'The first: it can\'t be 0. So choose it first: 9 ways.'],
            ['Second digit?', 'Any digit except the first, including 0: 9 ways.'],
            ['Third digit?', 'Any except the two used: 8 ways.'],
            '9 × 9 × 8 = <b>648</b>. Choosing the last digit first would go wrong: the number of options for the first digit would then depend on whether 0 was already used.',
          ] },
        ] },
        { h: 'Permutations: order matters', blocks: [
          `<p>Arranging n different things in a row: n! = n × (n − 1) × … × 1 ways. Choosing an ordered list of k from n: n × (n − 1) × … × (n − k + 1) = n!/(n − k)!.</p>
           <p>Two standard twists. <b>Round tables:</b> rotating everyone one seat gives the same seating, so n people round a table have n!/n = (n − 1)! arrangements (equivalently, fix one person and arrange the rest). <b>"Must sit together":</b> glue the pair into one block, arrange the n − 1 units, then multiply by 2 for the order inside the block. <b>"Must not sit together":</b> count all arrangements and subtract the "together" ones.</p>`,
          { q: 'In how many ways can gold, silver and bronze go to 8 runners?', a: 336, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: '8 choices for gold, then 7, then 6.', sol: 'Order matters (gold differs from silver): 8 choices for gold, then 7 for silver, then 6 for bronze. 8 × 7 × 6 = <b>336</b>, which is 8!/5!.' },
          { worked: 'together and apart', q: 'Six people sit in a row. In how many arrangements are Ann and Bob next to each other? In how many are they apart?', steps: [
            ['Treat Ann and Bob as one block. How many units are there?', '5 units: the block and 4 other people. They can be arranged in 5! = 120 ways.'],
            ['Inside the block?', 'AB or BA: 2 ways. So together: 120 × 2 = 240.'],
            ['Apart?', 'All arrangements minus together: 720 − 240 = <b>480</b>.'],
            'Check with probability: Bob sits next to Ann with probability 240/720 = 1/3. (Ann at an end has one neighbour seat, in the middle two; averaged over her 6 seats that\'s 10 of 30 seat choices for Bob.)',
          ] },
        ] },
        { h: 'Combinations: order doesn\'t matter', blocks: [
          `<p>Choosing a <i>set</i> of k from n: C(n, k) = n!/(k!(n − k)!). Each set of k appears k! times among the ordered lists, which is why we divide by k!.</p>
           <p>Useful facts: C(n, k) = C(n, n − k), since choosing who's in is choosing who's out, and C(n, 2) = n(n − 1)/2 counts pairs.</p>`,
          { fig: 'strip', cell: 34, labelW: 92, rows: [{ label: 'ordered lists', cells: ['A', 'B', { gap: true }, 'B', 'A', { gap: true }, 'A', 'C', { gap: true }, 'C', 'A', { gap: true }, 'B', 'C', { gap: true }, 'C', 'B'] }, { label: 'sets', cells: [{ t: 'A', tone: 'acc' }, { t: 'B', tone: 'acc' }, { gap: true }, { gap: true }, { gap: true }, { gap: true }, { t: 'A', tone: 'acc' }, { t: 'C', tone: 'acc' }, { gap: true }, { gap: true }, { gap: true }, { gap: true }, { t: 'B', tone: 'acc' }, { t: 'C', tone: 'acc' }] }], wide: true, alt: 'Six ordered pairs from A, B and C collapse into three unordered sets.', cap: 'Each set of 2 appears 2! times among the ordered pairs: 6/2 = 3 = C(3, 2).' },
          `<p>Computing them fast: cancel before multiplying. C(10, 3) = (10 × 9 × 8)/(3 × 2 × 1) = 120; never write out 10!. Know a few by heart: C(52, 5) = 2,598,960, C(10, 5) = 252, C(6, 3) = 20, C(2n, n) ≈ 4ⁿ/√(πn).</p>
           <p><b>Subsets and the binomial theorem.</b> Each of n items is either in or out, so there are 2ⁿ subsets in total, and C(n, 0) + C(n, 1) + … + C(n, n) = 2ⁿ. The same numbers are the coefficients in (x + y)ⁿ = Σ C(n, k)x<sup>k</sup>y<sup>n−k</sup>: to get x<sup>k</sup> you pick x from k of the n brackets. That's why they're called binomial coefficients, and why the number of heads in n flips has P(k) = C(n, k)/2ⁿ.</p>`,
          { q: 'Six people each shake hands once with everyone else. How many handshakes?', a: 15, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: 'Each handshake is a pair of people.', sol: 'Each handshake is an unordered pair of people: C(6, 2) = 6 × 5/2 = <b>15</b>. Another way: each of 6 people shakes 5 hands, 30 in all, but that counts every handshake twice (once from each end), so 15.' },
          { id: 'a1', q: 'How many 5-card hands are there from a 52-card deck?', a: 2598960, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: 'C(52, 5). Work it out as (52 × 51 × 50 × 49 × 48)/120.', sol: 'Order doesn\'t matter in a hand, so C(52, 5) = (52 × 51 × 50 × 49 × 48)/(5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1). Cancel first: 52/4 = 13, 50/(5 × 2) = 5, 48/3 = 16, leaving 13 × 51 × 5 × 49 × 16 = <b>2,598,960</b>. Worth remembering.' },
          { id: 'm1', q: 'A committee of 3 is picked from 10 people. Then a chair is picked from the committee. Which product counts the outcomes?', opts: [['C(10, 3) × 3'], ['10 × 9 × 8', 'That treats all three roles as different, but only the chair is special.'], ['C(10, 3)', 'That leaves out the choice of chair.'], ['C(10, 3) × 3!', 'That orders all three members.']], sol: 'Choose the set, then the chair: 120 × 3 = 360. Also 10 × C(9, 2) = 360, picking the chair first: a nice check.' },
          { worked: 'counting into a probability', q: 'You are dealt 5 cards. What is the probability of exactly two aces?', steps: [
            ['How many hands have exactly two aces? Split the hand into aces and non-aces.', 'Choose 2 of the 4 aces, C(4, 2) = 6, and 3 of the 48 non-aces, C(48, 3) = 17,296.'],
            'Favourable hands: 6 × 17,296 = 103,776.',
            'P = 103,776/2,598,960 ≈ <b>0.040</b>, about 1 hand in 25.',
            'This "choose some of the special ones, the rest from the others" pattern is the <b>hypergeometric</b> distribution: P = C(K, k)C(N − K, n − k)/C(N, n).',
          ] },
        ] },
        { h: 'Repeated items and lattice paths', blocks: [
          `<p>To arrange items where some are identical, count as if all were different, then divide by the ways of shuffling the identical ones among themselves. MISSISSIPPI: 11!/(4! 4! 2!).</p>
           <p>A surprising number of problems are secretly "arrange letters". The classic is <b>lattice paths</b>: walking from one corner of a grid to the opposite one using only right (R) and up (U) steps. A route across a 4-by-3 grid is a word with 4 Rs and 3 Us, like RRURUUR, and every such word is a route. So there are 7!/(4! 3!) = C(7, 3) = 35 routes: choose which 3 of the 7 steps go up.</p>`,
          { q: 'How many distinct arrangements are there of the letters of BANANA?', a: 60, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: '6 letters: three As and two Ns.', sol: 'Pretend the letters are all different: 6! = 720. Swapping the three As among themselves (3! = 6 ways) or the two Ns (2 ways) gives the same word, so each word was counted 6 × 2 = 12 times. 720/12 = <b>60</b>.' },
          { id: 'x2', q: 'How many shortest routes are there along the grid lines from one corner of a 5-by-5 grid of squares to the opposite corner?', a: 252, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: 'Each route is 5 rights and 5 ups in some order.', sol: 'A route is a word of 5 Rs and 5 Us: choose which 5 of the 10 steps are Rs, C(10, 5) = <b>252</b>.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Stars and bars', blocks: [
          `<p>Splitting n identical items among k people (zero allowed) is the same as arranging n stars and k − 1 bars in a row. The bars mark where one person's share ends.</p>`,
          { fig: 'strip', cell: 26, rows: [{ cells: ['★', '★', '★', { t: '|', tone: 'acc' }, '★', { t: '|', tone: 'acc' }, '★', '★', '★'] }], alt: 'Seven stars and two bars, giving shares of 3, 1 and 3.', cap: '7 coins, 3 people: this row means shares of 3, 1 and 3. Every arrangement of 7 stars and 2 bars is one split, so there are C(9, 2) = 36.' },
          `<p>So the count is C(n + k − 1, k − 1). If everyone must get at least one, give each person one first, leaving n − k to split freely: C(n − 1, k − 1).</p>
           <p>The same count answers "how many whole-number solutions does x₁ + … + x<sub>k</sub> = n have with every x ≥ 0", and "how many ways can you choose n items from k types when repeats are allowed and order doesn't matter" (a bag of 10 sweets from 3 flavours: C(12, 2) = 66). Recognising the disguise is the hard part.</p>`,
          { q: 'In how many ways can 10 identical coins be split among 3 people (someone may get none)?', a: 66, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: '10 stars and 2 bars.', sol: 'Line up 10 stars and 2 bars; each arrangement is one split. There are 12 positions and we choose which 2 are bars: C(12, 2) = <b>66</b>.' },
          { id: 'a2', q: 'And if each person must get at least one coin?', a: 36, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: 'Hand out one each, then split the remaining 7.', sol: 'Give each person one coin first. The remaining 7 can be split freely: 7 stars and 2 bars, C(9, 2) = <b>36</b>. (Or: put the 2 bars in 2 of the 9 gaps between the 10 coins, so no share is empty.)' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Complements and inclusion–exclusion', blocks: [
          `<p>"At least one" is usually easiest as "all outcomes minus none". PINs with at least one repeated digit = all PINs − PINs with all digits different.</p>`,
          { q: 'How many 4-digit PINs (0000 to 9999) have at least one repeated digit?', a: 4960, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: 'Subtract the PINs with four different digits from 10,000.', sol: 'All PINs: 10⁴ = 10,000. All different: 10 × 9 × 8 × 7 = 5,040. So at least one repeat: 10,000 − 5,040 = <b>4,960</b>, nearly half. Direct counting (exactly one pair, two pairs, a triple…) would take five cases.' },
          `<p>When the thing to avoid is "any of several overlapping conditions", use <b>inclusion–exclusion</b>: subtract the outcomes breaking each condition, add back those breaking two at once, subtract those breaking three, and so on.</p>
           <p>The famous example is <b>derangements</b>: arrangements of n items where nothing is in its original place (n letters in n envelopes, every one wrong). Let A<sub>i</sub> be "item i is in its own place". Arrangements with a given set of j items fixed: (n − j)!. So</p>
           <div class="hb-eq">D<sub>n</sub> = n! − C(n, 1)(n − 1)! + C(n, 2)(n − 2)! − … = n! (1 − 1/1! + 1/2! − 1/3! + … ± 1/n!)</div>
           <p>The bracket is the start of the series for e<sup>−1</sup>, so the fraction of arrangements with no item in place tends to 1/e ≈ 0.368, very quickly. D<sub>4</sub> = 24(1 − 1 + ½ − ⅙ + 1/24) = 9.</p>`,
          { id: 'x3', q: 'Five letters are put into their five addressed envelopes at random. In how many of the 120 arrangements is every letter in the wrong envelope?', a: 44, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: '5!(1 − 1 + 1/2 − 1/6 + 1/24 − 1/120).', sol: '120 × (1/2 − 1/6 + 1/24 − 1/120) = 60 − 20 + 5 − 1 = <b>44</b>. That\'s 44/120 ≈ 0.367, already close to 1/e.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Counting poker hands', blocks: [
          `<p>Hands are the classic test of careful counting, and the safest method is to build the hand in stages, one decision at a time, and ask at each stage whether order matters.</p>`,
          { worked: 'a full house', q: 'How many 5-card hands are a full house (three of one rank, two of another)?', steps: [
            ['Stage 1: choose the rank of the triple. How many ways?', '13.'],
            ['Stage 2: choose 3 of its 4 suits.', 'C(4, 3) = 4.'],
            ['Stage 3: choose the rank of the pair. Why is this not C(12, 2)?', 'We need one rank from the remaining 12, so 12. The triple and the pair have different roles, so rank order matters here.'],
            ['Stage 4: choose 2 of its 4 suits.', 'C(4, 2) = 6.'],
            'Multiply the stages: 13 × 4 × 12 × 6 = 3,744 hands. That\'s about 0.14% of all hands.',
          ] },
          { trap: 'Double counting. For two pair, the two pair ranks play the same role, so choose them together with C(13, 2). Choosing 13 × 12 counts every hand twice. Ask: could I build the same hand in two ways?' },
          { id: 'a3', q: 'How many 5-card hands are two pair (two ranks with a pair each, and a fifth card of another rank)?', a: 123552, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: 'C(13, 2) for the ranks, 6 suits for each pair, then 44 for the fifth card.', sol: 'Ranks of the pairs: they play the same role, so C(13, 2) = 78. Suits for each pair: C(4, 2) = 6, twice. Fifth card: any of the 44 cards not of those two ranks. 78 × 6 × 6 × 44 = <b>123,552</b>, about 4.8% of hands.' },
          `<p>A self-check for any hand count: the probabilities of all the hand types must add to 1. The standard counts are: high card 1,302,540; one pair 1,098,240; two pair 123,552; three of a kind 54,912; straight 10,200; flush 5,108; full house 3,744; four of a kind 624; straight flush 40 (including the 4 royal flushes). They add to 2,598,960.</p>`,
        ] },
      ],
      takeaways: ['Stages multiply, as long as the number of options at each stage is fixed. Put restricted stages first.', 'Order matters: n!/(n − k)!. It doesn\'t: divide again by k!. Round tables: (n − 1)!.', 'Identical items: divide by their internal arrangements. Grid routes are words in R and U.', 'Identical items into boxes: stars and bars.', '"At least one": count the complement. Several overlapping conditions: inclusion–exclusion.', 'Derangements: about n!/e, so the chance nothing is in place tends to 1/e.', 'Same role means choose together; different roles means choose in turn.'],
      explain: { q: 'Why do we divide by k! to get from ordered lists to combinations?', a: 'Every set of k items can be written in k! different orders, so each set is counted exactly k! times among the ordered lists. Dividing by k! counts each set once. The same idea, "count something easier, then divide by how many times each answer was counted", also gives round tables (divide by n rotations) and words with repeated letters.' },
      homework: [
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'How many ways can 5 people sit in a row?', a: 120, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: '5!', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> fill the seats one at a time.</p><ol><li>Seat 1: 5 choices. Seat 2: 4 (anyone not yet seated). Then 3, 2, 1.</li><li>5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 5! = <b>120</b>.</li></ol>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'How many ways can 5 people sit around a round table, if rotations count as the same?', a: 24, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: 'Fix one person, then arrange the rest.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> rotations don\'t count, so fix one person\'s seat to remove them.</p><ol><li>Seat Alice anywhere; every seating can be rotated so she is in that seat.</li><li>Arrange the other 4 in the remaining seats: 4! = <b>24</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Check:</b> 5! = 120 row-style seatings, and each round-table seating appears 5 times (once per rotation): 120/5 = 24.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'How many distinct arrangements are there of the letters T, O, O, T?', a: 6, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: '4!/(2! 2!).', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> count as if all letters were different, then divide by the swaps of identical letters.</p><ol><li>4! = 24 arrangements of four distinct letters.</li><li>Each word is counted 2! × 2! = 4 times (swap the Ts, swap the Os).</li><li>24/4 = <b>6</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Check by listing:</b> TTOO, TOTO, TOOT, OTTO, OTOT, OOTT. Six. (Equivalently, choose the 2 positions for the Ts: C(4, 2) = 6.)</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'How many 5-card hands contain exactly 3 hearts?', a: 211926, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: 'Choose the hearts, then the non-hearts.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> split the hand into its hearts and its non-hearts, and choose each part.</p><ol><li>3 of the 13 hearts: C(13, 3) = 286.</li><li>2 of the 39 non-hearts: C(39, 2) = 741.</li><li>286 × 741 = <b>211,926</b>.</li></ol><p><b>As a probability:</b> 211,926/2,598,960 ≈ 8.2%.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'How many non-negative whole solutions does a + b + c + d = 6 have?', a: 84, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: '6 stars and 3 bars.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> stars and bars: 6 units shared among 4 variables.</p><ol><li>Arrange 6 stars and 3 bars (4 variables need 3 dividers): 9 positions.</li><li>Choose which 3 are bars: C(9, 3) = <b>84</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Check a small case:</b> a + b = 2 has 3 solutions (0+2, 1+1, 2+0), and the formula gives C(3, 1) = 3.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'Three dice are rolled. How many of the 216 outcomes have all three faces different?', a: 120, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: '6 × 5 × 4.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> the dice are distinguishable (first, second, third), so use ordered stages.</p><ol><li>First die: 6 faces. Second must differ: 5. Third must differ from both: 4.</li><li>6 × 5 × 4 = <b>120</b>, so P(all different) = 120/216 = 5/9.</li></ol><p><b>The rest:</b> 6 triples and 216 − 120 − 6 = 90 outcomes with exactly a pair.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'How many 5-card hands are a flush, including straight flushes?', a: 5148, tol: { abs: 0.5 }, hint: 'Choose the suit, then 5 of its 13 cards.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> two stages: the suit, then the cards.</p><ol><li>Suit: 4 choices.</li><li>5 of its 13 cards: C(13, 5) = 1,287.</li><li>4 × 1,287 = <b>5,148</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Note:</b> 40 of these are straight flushes, which is why poker tables list a flush as 5,108.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'stretch', q: 'Prove C(n, k) = C(n − 1, k − 1) + C(n − 1, k) by a counting argument, without algebra.', sol: '<p><b>Proof by counting committees.</b> Both sides count the committees of k chosen from n people.</p><ol><li>Single out one person, Alice.</li><li>Committees that include Alice: choose her other k − 1 colleagues from the remaining n − 1, C(n − 1, k − 1) ways.</li><li>Committees without Alice: choose all k from the other n − 1, C(n − 1, k) ways.</li><li>Every committee is in exactly one of these groups, so the counts add to C(n, k).</li></ol><p>This is the rule that builds Pascal\'s triangle, each entry the sum of the two above. Proofs like this, counting one set in two ways, are often far shorter than algebra.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'lab', q: 'Deal yourself 30 five-card hands from a real deck and count how many contain at least one pair. Then work out the exact chance: 1 − (no pair) = 1 − [C(13, 5) × 4⁵]/C(52, 5). How close were you?', sol: '<p><b>Exact:</b> a no-pair hand has 5 different ranks, C(13, 5) = 1,287 ways, each in any suit, 4⁵ = 1,024: 1,317,888 hands, or 50.7%. So at least a pair (or anything better) is about 49.3%, roughly 15 of your 30 hands.</p><p><b>How close should you be?</b> The count of 30 hands with a pair has a standard deviation of √(30 × 0.49 × 0.51) ≈ 2.7, so anything from about 10 to 20 is normal.</p>' },
      ],
      practice: [['Combinatorics interview questions', '#/search/combinatorics'], ['Cards topic', '#/topic/cards']],
    },

    'prob-basics': {
      title: 'Probability basics', minutes: 45,
      why: 'Probability is a language for uncertainty, and trading is the business of putting a price on uncertainty. These few rules underlie everything else in this handbook, and most interview mistakes come from breaking one of them without noticing.',
      goals: ['Turn a problem into equally likely outcomes, and know when outcomes are not equally likely', 'Use the complement, addition and multiplication rules, and inclusion–exclusion for three events', 'Tell independent events from mutually exclusive ones, and test for independence', 'Handle draws with and without replacement, including the "any position is the same" symmetry'],
      prereq: ['counting'],
      sections: [
        { h: 'Sample spaces and equally likely outcomes', blocks: [
          `<p>A <b>sample space</b> is the list of everything that could happen, and an <b>event</b> is a subset of it. When every outcome is equally likely, P(event) = (outcomes in the event)/(all outcomes).</p>
           <p>The skill is choosing outcomes that really <i>are</i> equally likely. With two dice, use the 36 ordered pairs (first die, second die), not the 11 totals: a total of 7 can happen 6 ways, a total of 2 only 1 way.</p>
           <p>A reliable habit: <b>imagine the objects are labelled</b>, even when they look identical. Paint one die red and one blue. Then "a 3 and a 4" is two outcomes (red 3, blue 4) and (red 4, blue 3), while "two 3s" is only one. Forgetting the labels is exactly the mistake that makes people think a 3-and-4 is as likely as a double 3. Nature doesn't care whether you can tell the dice apart.</p>
           <p>When outcomes aren't equally likely, you can't just count. You either weight each outcome by its probability, or go back to a finer description where they are equal. "0, 1 or 2 heads" isn't equally likely; HH, HT, TH, TT is.</p>`,
          { fig: 'dice', mark: (a, b) => (a + b === 7 ? 'acc' : ''), show: (a, b) => a + b, alt: 'A six by six grid of two-dice outcomes with the six cells that total 7 highlighted.', cap: 'The 36 outcomes, labelled with their totals. The six that total 7 run along a diagonal.' },
          `<p>Read the grid as a picture of the distribution of the total: each diagonal is one total, and its length is the number of ways. Diagonals grow from 1 cell (total 2) to 6 cells (total 7) and shrink back to 1 (total 12). So P(total = k) = (6 − |k − 7|)/36 for k = 2…12. Learn that formula; it saves counting in every dice question.</p>`,
          { widget: 'dice', predict: { q: 'You roll two dice 10,000 times. Which total comes up most often, and roughly how often?', opts: ['7, about 1 time in 6', '7, about 1 time in 11', 'All totals equally often', '6 and 8 tie for first'], then: 'Now roll them and see.' } },
          { q: 'Two dice are rolled. What is the probability their total is 7?', a: 1 / 6, hint: 'Count the ordered pairs that add to 7.', sol: 'Ordered pairs that add to 7: (1,6), (2,5), (3,4), (4,3), (5,2), (6,1). That\'s 6 of the 36 equally likely pairs, so <b>1/6</b>. Every first die has exactly one partner that makes 7, which is why 7 is the most likely total.' },
          { q: 'Two dice: what is the probability they show the same number?', a: 1 / 6, hint: 'Whatever the first die shows, what must the second do?', sol: 'Whatever the first die shows, the second must match it, which happens 1 time in 6: <b>1/6</b>. (Counting gives the same: 6 doubles out of 36 pairs.) Conditioning on the first die is often quicker than counting the whole grid.' },
          { id: 'm1', q: 'Two coins are flipped. Someone says the outcomes are "0, 1 or 2 heads", so P(1 head) = 1/3. What is wrong?', opts: [['The three aren\'t equally likely'], ['There are four totals, not three', 'There are three totals. Are they equally likely?'], ['Coins are not independent', 'They are.'], ['Nothing, 1/3 is right', 'List HH, HT, TH, TT.']], sol: 'The equally likely outcomes are HH, HT, TH and TT. One head covers two of them (HT and TH), so P = 2/4 = 1/2, not 1/3. Labelling the coins (first, second) is what reveals the two ways.' },
          { worked: 'the dice-total formula in action', q: 'Two dice are rolled. What is the probability the total is 5 or 9?', steps: [
            ['How many ways to make 5?', '6 − |5 − 7| = 4: (1,4), (2,3), (3,2), (4,1).'],
            ['And 9?', '6 − |9 − 7| = 4: (3,6), (4,5), (5,4), (6,3).'],
            ['Can the total be both 5 and 9?', 'No, so add: 4 + 4 = 8 ways.'],
            'P = 8/36 = <b>2/9</b>. Totals equally far from 7 are equally likely, so you only ever need to count one side.',
          ] },
        ] },
        { h: 'The three rules everything rests on', blocks: [
          `<p>All of probability follows from three rules: every probability is between 0 and 1; the whole sample space has probability 1; and for events that can't happen together, probabilities add. Everything in this chapter is a consequence:</p>
           <ul><li><b>Complement:</b> A and "not A" can't both happen and together cover everything, so P(A) + P(not A) = 1.</li>
           <li><b>Addition:</b> split "A or B" into pieces that don't overlap (A only, B only, both) and add them.</li>
           <li><b>Multiplication:</b> P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B | A): the chance A happens, times the chance B then happens given A. Independence is the special case where P(B | A) = P(B).</li></ul>
           <p>When a question looks unfamiliar, ask which of these it is really asking. "At least one" is a complement question; "either … or" is addition; "both", "all" or "in a row" is multiplication.</p>`,
        ] },
        { h: 'The complement rule', blocks: [
          `<p>P(not A) = 1 − P(A). It turns "at least one" problems into "none" problems, which usually multiply cleanly.</p>
           <p>Why it works so often: "at least one success in n tries" splits into many messy cases (exactly 1, exactly 2, …), but its opposite, "no successes at all", is a single case. If the tries are independent with success chance p, P(none) = (1 − p)<sup>n</sup>, so P(at least one) = 1 − (1 − p)<sup>n</sup>.</p>
           <p>Two approximations worth knowing. When np is small, 1 − (1 − p)<sup>n</sup> ≈ np: ten tries at 1% is about 10% (exactly 9.6%). When n = 1/p, the answer is about 1 − 1/e ≈ 63%: try a 1-in-100 shot 100 times and you succeed about 63% of the time, not 100%.</p>`,
          { q: 'You roll a die 4 times. What is the probability of at least one six?', a: 1 - (5 / 6) ** 4, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'Find the chance of no sixes in 4 rolls first.', sol: 'Each roll misses a six with probability 5/6, and the rolls are independent, so P(no six in 4) = (5/6)⁴ = 625/1296. The complement: 1 − 625/1296 = 671/1296 ≈ <b>0.518</b>. Slightly better than evens: a famous 17th-century gambling bet. (The naive "4 × 1/6 = 2/3" double-counts rolls with two or more sixes.)' },
          { worked: 'the birthday problem', q: 'How many people must be in a room for a shared birthday to be more likely than not? Assume 365 equally likely birthdays.', steps: [
            ['"Some pair matches" is messy. What is its complement?', 'Everyone has a different birthday.'],
            ['Line people up. What is the chance each new person misses everyone before them?', '364/365 for the second, 363/365 for the third, and so on.'],
            'So P(no match among n) = (364/365)(363/365)…((366 − n)/365).',
            ['Why does it drop faster than you\'d expect?', 'Because it counts pairs, not people: 23 people make C(23, 2) = 253 pairs, each a chance of 1/365.'],
            'A quick estimate: P(no match) ≈ e<sup>−(pairs)/365</sup> = e<sup>−253/365</sup> ≈ e<sup>−0.69</sup> ≈ 0.50. The exact product at n = 23 is 0.493.',
            'At n = 23 the product falls just below ½, so <b>23 people</b> is enough.',
          ] },
          { worked: 'how many tries?', q: 'A strategy finds a profitable trade on any given day with probability 5%, independently. How many days until you are at least 90% sure of having found at least one?', steps: [
            ['Write the requirement with the complement.', '1 − 0.95ⁿ ≥ 0.9, so 0.95ⁿ ≤ 0.1.'],
            ['Solve for n with logs.', 'n ≥ ln 0.1/ln 0.95 = (−2.303)/(−0.0513) ≈ 44.9.'],
            'So <b>45 days</b>. The naive "0.05n ≥ 0.9, so 18 days" is badly wrong: after 18 days you are only 1 − 0.95¹⁸ ≈ 60% sure.',
          ] },
          { id: 'x1', q: 'You buy one ticket a week in a raffle you win with probability 1/50 each week, independently. What is the probability you win at least once in 50 weeks? (Give a decimal.)', a: 1 - (49 / 50) ** 50, tol: { abs: 0.003 }, hint: 'Complement: lose all 50 weeks.', sol: 'P(lose every week) = (49/50)⁵⁰ ≈ 0.364, so P(win at least once) ≈ <b>0.636</b>. That\'s the "n = 1/p gives about 1 − 1/e ≈ 0.632" rule.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'The addition rule and inclusion–exclusion', blocks: [
          `<p>P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B). Subtract the overlap, or you count it twice. If A and B can't both happen (<b>mutually exclusive</b>), the overlap is zero.</p>`,
          { fig: 'venn', a: 'Hearts', b: 'Kings', both: 'K♥', shade: 'union', out: '52 cards', alt: 'Two overlapping circles for hearts and kings, with the king of hearts in the overlap.', cap: 'Add hearts and kings, and the king of hearts gets counted twice, so take it off once.' },
          { q: 'A card is drawn from a standard deck. What is the probability it is a heart or a king?', a: 4 / 13, hint: '13 hearts, 4 kings, and one card is both.', sol: 'Hearts: 13 cards. Kings: 4 cards. The king of hearts is in both, so adding gives 17 but counts it twice. Take it off once: (13 + 4 − 1)/52 = 16/52 = <b>4/13</b>.' },
          `<p>With three events the pattern continues: add the singles, subtract the pairs, add back the triple.</p>
           <p style="text-align:center">P(A ∪ B ∪ C) = P(A) + P(B) + P(C) − P(AB) − P(AC) − P(BC) + P(ABC)</p>
           <p>Why add the triple back? An outcome in all three is counted 3 times by the singles, then removed 3 times by the pairs, leaving it uncounted. Adding P(ABC) once restores it. The same alternating pattern works for any number of events, and it is the standard way to count "at least one of several overlapping things".</p>`,
          { worked: 'inclusion–exclusion with three sets', q: 'How many whole numbers from 1 to 100 are divisible by 2, 3 or 5?', steps: [
            ['Count each set alone.', 'By 2: 50. By 3: 33. By 5: 20. Total 103, too many.'],
            ['Count the pairs (divisible by both means divisible by the product, as they share no factors).', 'By 6: 16. By 10: 10. By 15: 6. Total 32.'],
            ['And all three?', 'By 30: 3.'],
            '103 − 32 + 3 = <b>74</b>. So 26 numbers up to 100 are divisible by none of them.',
          ] },
          { id: 'x2', q: 'A card is drawn. What is the probability it is a spade, a face card (J, Q, K) or an ace?', a: 25 / 52, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'Spades 13, faces 12, aces 4. Then the overlaps: spade faces, the ace of spades. Faces and aces never overlap.', sol: 'Singles: 13 + 12 + 4 = 29. Pairs: spade and face 3, spade and ace 1, face and ace 0. Triple: 0. So 29 − 4 + 0 = 25 cards: <b>25/52</b> ≈ 0.481.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Independence', blocks: [
          `<p>A and B are <b>independent</b> if knowing one happened doesn't change the chance of the other; then P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B).</p>
           <p>Independence is a property you <b>check</b> or <b>assume</b>, never one you can eyeball. Separate coin flips or dice are independent by construction. Events about the same draw often aren't, but sometimes they surprisingly are: on one card, "heart" and "king" are independent, because P(heart and king) = 1/52 = (13/52)(4/52). Knowing it's a king leaves the suit at 1 in 4.</p>
           <p>In trading terms, independence is the assumption that lets you multiply, and it's the one most likely to be wrong. Daily returns, defaults in a portfolio, and failures of "independent" safety systems are often linked, so multiplied-out probabilities of joint disasters come out far too small.</p>`,
          { trap: 'Independent is not the same as mutually exclusive. Two events that can\'t happen together are very dependent: if one happens, the other certainly didn\'t.' },
          { q: 'A fair coin is flipped three times. What is the probability of heads, heads, tails, in that order?', a: 1 / 8, hint: 'Flips are independent: multiply.', sol: 'The flips are independent, so multiply: ½ × ½ × ½ = <b>1/8</b>. Every specific sequence of three flips has the same 1/8, including HHH. A sequence that "looks random" isn\'t more likely than one that doesn\'t.' },
          { q: 'Three flips: what is the probability of at least one head?', a: 7 / 8, hint: 'Complement.', sol: 'The only way to get no head is TTT, which has probability 1/8. So P(at least one head) = 1 − 1/8 = <b>7/8</b>.' },
          { id: 'm2', q: 'A fair coin lands heads five times in a row. What is the chance of heads on the next flip?', opts: [['1/2: no memory'], ['Less than 1/2: tails is due', 'That\'s the gambler\'s fallacy. Past flips don\'t affect the next one.'], ['1/64', 'That\'s the chance of six heads in a row, before any flips.'], ['1/32', 'That\'s the chance of the five heads you already saw.']], sol: 'If the coin is fair, the flips are independent. (After 50 heads in a row, though, you\'d start to doubt it is fair. That\'s Bayes, in the next chapter.)' },
          `<p><b>Pairwise is not enough.</b> Several events are mutually independent only if every subset multiplies. Flip two coins and let A = "first is heads", B = "second is heads", C = "they match". Each has probability ½, each pair multiplies (P(A and C) = P(HH) = ¼), yet P(A and B and C) = P(HH) = ¼, not ⅛. Knowing A and B tells you C for certain. Interviewers like this example because it breaks the "check pairs and multiply" habit.</p>`,
          { id: 'x3', q: 'Two dice. Let A = "the first die is even" and B = "the total is 7". Are A and B independent?', opts: [['Yes: P(A and B) = 1/12 = P(A) × P(B)'], ['No: the total depends on the first die', 'It does in general, but for a total of 7 every first value has exactly one partner.'], ['No: they are mutually exclusive', 'Both can happen, e.g. (2, 5).'], ['Only if the dice are fair and identical', 'Fair dice are assumed throughout.']], sol: 'P(A) = 1/2 and P(B) = 1/6. A and B together: (2,5), (4,3), (6,1), which is 3 of 36 = 1/12 = (1/2)(1/6). So they are independent, which is not obvious: knowing the first die tells you nothing about whether the total is 7. For a total of 8 it would: an even first die makes 8 more likely (3 of 18 against 2 of 18).' },
        ] },
        { h: 'With and without replacement', blocks: [
          `<p>Drawing <b>with replacement</b> (put it back) keeps every draw identical and independent. Drawing <b>without replacement</b> changes the odds as you go, so multiply conditional probabilities along the way: P(two aces) = (4/52)(3/51).</p>
           <p>One idea makes many "without replacement" questions trivial: <b>by symmetry, every position in a shuffled deck looks the same</b>. The 2nd card, the 10th card and the last card are each equally likely to be any of the 52 cards. So P(the 2nd card is an ace) = 4/52, without worrying about what the first card was.</p>
           <p>You can confirm it the long way: P(2nd is ace) = P(1st ace)P(2nd ace | 1st ace) + P(1st not)P(2nd ace | 1st not) = (4/52)(3/51) + (48/52)(4/51) = 204/2652 = 4/52. Symmetry gives it in one line.</p>`,
          { worked: 'symmetry instead of cases', q: 'You deal cards from a shuffled deck until the first ace appears. What is the probability that the card right after the first ace is the ace of spades?', steps: [
            ['This looks like it needs a sum over where the first ace is. Try something cleverer: take the ace of spades out and shuffle the other 51.', 'Now put the ace of spades back in one of the 52 gaps, uniformly at random.'],
            ['When is it right after the first ace (of the 51)?', 'Exactly when it lands in the one gap just after that ace.'],
            'So the probability is <b>1/52</b>, the same as for any specific position. The same argument says the card after the first ace is the 2 of clubs with probability 1/52 too.',
          ] },
          { id: 'x4', q: 'An urn has 5 red and 3 blue balls. You draw three without replacement. What is the probability that the third ball is blue?', a: 3 / 8, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'By symmetry, the third ball is equally likely to be any of the 8.', sol: 'Every position is alike, so the third ball is blue with the same probability as the first: <b>3/8</b>. No case-splitting needed.' },
        ] },
      ],
      takeaways: ['Pick outcomes that are genuinely equally likely: label the objects (ordered pairs, not totals).', 'Two dice: P(total = k) = (6 − |k − 7|)/36.', '"At least one": 1 − P(none). n tries at 1/n gives about 63%, not 100%.', 'Or: add, subtract the overlaps, add back the triple overlap.', 'Independent ≠ mutually exclusive, and pairwise independent ≠ independent.', 'Without replacement, every position in a shuffled deck is alike.', 'Coins have no memory.'],
      explain: { q: 'Explain the difference between mutually exclusive and independent, using one card example of each.', a: '"Heart" and "club" on one card are mutually exclusive: both can\'t happen, so knowing one tells you the other didn\'t. "Heart" and "king" are independent: P(heart | king) = 1/4 = P(heart), so knowing the rank tells you nothing about the suit. A good answer adds that mutually exclusive events (with non-zero chances) are never independent: learning one happened rules the other out.' },
      homework: [
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'Two dice: what is the probability the total is at least 10?', a: 1 / 6, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'Count totals of 10, 11 and 12.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> count ordered pairs, using P(total = k) = (6 − |k − 7|)/36.</p><ol><li>Total 10: 3 ways, (4,6), (5,5), (6,4).</li><li>Total 11: 2 ways, (5,6), (6,5).</li><li>Total 12: 1 way, (6,6).</li><li>3 + 2 + 1 = 6 of 36: <b>1/6</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Check:</b> by symmetry about 7, "at least 10" matches "at most 4" (totals 2, 3, 4: 1 + 2 + 3 = 6 ways). Same answer.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'Two dice: what is the probability that at least one shows a 6?', a: 11 / 36, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'Complement.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> "at least one" means use the complement.</p><ol><li>P(no 6 on a die) = 5/6, and the dice are independent, so P(no 6 on either) = (5/6)² = 25/36.</li><li>P(at least one 6) = 1 − 25/36 = <b>11/36</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Check by counting:</b> 6 pairs have a 6 first, 6 have a 6 second, and (6,6) is in both: 6 + 6 − 1 = 11. The trap is adding 1/6 + 1/6 = 12/36, which counts (6,6) twice.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'A card is drawn. What is the probability it is a red card or a face card (J, Q, K)?', a: 8 / 13, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: '26 red, 12 faces, 6 are both.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> addition rule, removing the overlap.</p><ol><li>Red cards: 26. Face cards: 3 per suit × 4 suits = 12.</li><li>Red face cards (counted in both): 3 hearts + 3 diamonds = 6.</li><li>(26 + 12 − 6)/52 = 32/52 = <b>8/13</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Check:</b> count the complement, black non-face cards: 2 suits × 10 = 20. 52 − 20 = 32. Same.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'A coin is flipped 10 times. What is the probability that all ten flips match?', a: 2 / 1024, tol: { abs: 0.0002 }, hint: 'Two outcomes of 2¹⁰.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> count equally likely sequences.</p><ol><li>There are 2¹⁰ = 1024 equally likely sequences.</li><li>"All match" means all heads or all tails: 2 sequences.</li><li>2/1024 = <b>1/512</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Another way:</b> the first flip can be anything; each of the other 9 must match it, with probability ½ each: (½)⁹ = 1/512. Fixing the first outcome and asking the rest to follow is a common shortcut.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'Three cards are drawn without replacement. What is the probability they are all of different suits?', a: (52 * 39 * 26) / (52 * 51 * 50), tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'The first card can be anything. How many cards suit the second, then the third?', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> multiply conditional probabilities card by card.</p><ol><li>The first card can be anything: probability 1.</li><li>The second must avoid the first card\'s suit: 39 of the remaining 51.</li><li>The third must avoid both suits so far: 26 of the remaining 50.</li><li>1 × 39/51 × 26/50 = 1014/2550 ≈ <b>0.398</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Check by counting:</b> choose 3 suits of 4 (4 ways) and one card from each (13³): 4 × 2197 = 8788 hands, out of C(52, 3) = 22100. 8788/22100 ≈ 0.398.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'What is the probability that 2 people out of 5 share a birth month, assuming 12 equally likely months?', a: 1 - (11 * 10 * 9 * 8) / 12 ** 4, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'Complement: all five months differ.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> the birthday problem with 12 "days". Find P(all different) and subtract.</p><ol><li>The first person can have any month. The second must avoid it (11/12), the third avoids two (10/12), then 9/12, then 8/12.</li><li>P(all different) = (11 × 10 × 9 × 8)/12⁴ = 7920/20736 ≈ 0.382.</li><li>P(some pair shares) = 1 − 0.382 ≈ <b>0.618</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Sense check:</b> 5 people make C(5, 2) = 10 pairs, each matching with probability 1/12, so you expect 10/12 ≈ 0.83 matching pairs. More than half a chance of at least one is reasonable.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'stretch', q: 'Show that if A and B are independent, so are A and "not B".', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> split A by whether B happened, then use independence.</p><ol><li>A happens either with B or without it, and these can\'t overlap: P(A) = P(A and B) + P(A and not B).</li><li>So P(A and not B) = P(A) − P(A and B) = P(A) − P(A)P(B), using independence.</li><li>= P(A)(1 − P(B)) = P(A)P(not B), which is exactly the definition of A and "not B" being independent.</li></ol><p><b>Why it matters:</b> independence is a statement about information. If learning B tells you nothing about A, learning that B didn\'t happen can\'t tell you anything either.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'lab', q: 'Flip a coin 100 times and write the sequence down. Find your longest run of heads or tails. Now write a sequence of 100 "fake" flips out of your head, on another day. Which has the longer runs?', sol: '<p>Real sequences of 100 flips usually have a longest run of 6 or 7 (a good rule: about log₂ 100 ≈ 6.6). People faking randomness alternate too much and avoid long runs, typically stopping at 4.</p><p><b>Why it matters:</b> streaks in noise are normal. A trader with five losing days in a row, or a signal with a run of good months, is often just what randomness looks like. Before reading meaning into a streak, ask how long a streak pure chance would produce.</p>' },
      ],
      practice: [['Dice topic', '#/topic/dice'], ['Cards topic', '#/topic/cards'], ['Probability interview questions', '#/search/probability']],
    },

    conditional: {
      title: 'Conditional probability and Bayes', minutes: 50,
      why: 'Trading is updating beliefs. Every trade you see, and every number that prints, is evidence. Bayes tells you exactly how far that evidence should move you, which is why it matters more than any other idea in this part.',
      goals: ['Compute P(A | B) by shrinking the sample space', 'Use trees, the chain rule and the law of total probability', 'Update beliefs with Bayes, in natural frequencies and in odds', 'Avoid the base-rate fallacy, and see why evidence depends on how it was produced', 'Tell independence from conditional independence'],
      prereq: ['prob-basics'],
      sections: [
        { h: 'Conditioning shrinks the world', blocks: [
          `<p>P(A | B) = P(A and B)/P(B): restrict attention to the outcomes where B happened, and ask what fraction of those also have A.</p>
           <p>Read the formula as a recipe. Being told "B happened" deletes every outcome outside B. What's left still has the same <i>relative</i> likelihoods, so you rescale it to add up to 1, which is what dividing by P(B) does. The answer is the share of that smaller world where A also holds.</p>
           <p>With equally likely outcomes, that's just counting: P(A | B) = (outcomes in both)/(outcomes in B). You never need the formula if you can list the shrunken world.</p>`,
          { fig: 'dice', mark: (a, b) => (a + b === 8 ? (a % 2 === 0 ? 'ok' : 'acc') : ''), show: (a, b) => a + b, alt: 'The two-dice grid with the five cells that total 8 highlighted, three of which have both dice even.', cap: 'Given a total of 8, only 5 cells remain. Your world is now those cells.' },
          { q: 'Two dice sum to 8. What is the probability both are even?', a: 3 / 5, hint: 'List the ordered pairs that sum to 8.', sol: 'Given a total of 8, only five equally likely pairs remain: (2,6), (3,5), (4,4), (5,3), (6,2). Both dice are even in (2,6), (4,4) and (6,2): 3 of these 5, so <b>3/5</b>. (Unconditionally, both even is only 1/4: the information made it much more likely, because an even total needs the dice to be both odd or both even.)' },
          { trap: 'P(A | B) is not P(B | A). The chance that a winning trader had a good system isn\'t the chance a good system wins. Confusing them is the most common error in reasoning about evidence.' },
          `<p>A concrete case of the trap: nearly every successful hedge fund manager takes big risks (P(big risks | successful) is high). That says little about P(successful | big risks), which can be low, because many risk-takers blow up and you never hear from them. Whenever you read a statistic, ask which way round it is conditioned.</p>`,
        ] },
        { h: 'Trees: multiply along branches', blocks: [
          `<p>P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B | A). For draws without replacement, each stage's probability depends on what came before.</p>
           <p>With more stages it chains: P(A and B and C) = P(A) × P(B | A) × P(C | A and B). This <b>chain rule</b> is how you handle any "this, then that, then the other" sequence. Draw a tree whose levels are the stages; each branch is labelled with its probability <i>given everything above it</i>, and the probability of a whole path is the product along it.</p>`,
          { q: 'Two cards are dealt from a shuffled deck. What is the probability both are aces?', a: 1 / 221, tol: { abs: 0.0002 }, hint: 'First an ace (4 of 52), then an ace from what is left.', sol: 'First card an ace: 4/52. Given that, 3 aces are left among 51 cards: 3/51. Multiply along the branch: (4/52)(3/51) = 12/2652 = <b>1/221</b>. (Counting agrees: C(4, 2)/C(52, 2) = 6/1326.)' },
          { worked: 'a three-stage chain', q: 'You deal cards one at a time. What is the probability that the first ace is the third card?', steps: [
            ['Write it as a sequence of events.', 'First card not an ace, second not an ace, third an ace.'],
            ['Chance the first is not an ace?', '48/52.'],
            ['Then the second is not an ace, given the first wasn\'t?', '47/51: 47 non-aces remain among 51 cards.'],
            ['Then the third is an ace?', '4/50: all 4 aces remain among 50 cards.'],
            'Multiply: (48/52)(47/51)(4/50) = 9024/132600 ≈ <b>0.068</b>.',
          ] },
          { id: 'x1', q: 'Three cards are dealt. What is the probability they are all hearts? (Give a decimal.)', a: (13 / 52) * (12 / 51) * (11 / 50), tol: { abs: 0.0005 }, hint: 'Chain: 13/52, then 12/51, then 11/50.', sol: 'Chain rule: (13/52)(12/51)(11/50) = 1716/132600 ≈ <b>0.0129</b>. Check by counting: C(13, 3)/C(52, 3) = 286/22100, the same.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'The law of total probability', blocks: [
          `<p>If one of several situations must hold, P(A) = Σ P(situation) × P(A | situation). Example: urn X holds 2 red and 1 blue ball, urn Y holds 1 red and 3 blue. Pick an urn at random, then a ball.</p>`,
          { fig: 'tree', colW: 90, leafW: 130, root: { kids: [{ p: '1/2', t: 'X', kids: [{ p: '2/3', t: 'red', tone: 'bad' }, { p: '1/3', t: 'blue' }] }, { p: '1/2', t: 'Y', kids: [{ p: '1/4', t: 'red', tone: 'bad' }, { p: '3/4', t: 'blue' }] }] }, alt: 'A tree: pick urn X or Y with probability one half, then a red or blue ball.', cap: 'Multiply along a path, and add the paths that end in what you want.' },
          `<p>The law is a weighted average: the chance of red is the average of each urn's chance of red, weighted by how likely you are to be using that urn. It's the main tool for problems where something unknown (which urn, which coin, which type of trader) sits behind what you observe.</p>
           <p>The situations must <b>cover every possibility and not overlap</b>. If you forget a branch, or count one twice, the weights no longer add to 1 and the answer is wrong. Checking that the first-level branch probabilities sum to 1 is a quick safeguard.</p>`,
          { q: 'In that set-up, what is the probability of drawing red?', a: 11 / 24, hint: 'Average the two urns\' chances of red.', sol: 'Weight each urn\'s chance of red by the chance of picking that urn: ½ × 2/3 + ½ × 1/4 = 1/3 + 1/8 = 8/24 + 3/24 = <b>11/24</b>. It sits between 1/4 and 2/3, as an average must.' },
          { q: 'You drew red. What is the probability it came from urn X?', a: 8 / 11, hint: 'The share of the red probability that came via urn X.', sol: 'Of the total red probability 11/24, the path through urn X contributes ½ × 2/3 = 1/3 = 8/24. So P(X | red) = (8/24)/(11/24) = <b>8/11</b>. That\'s Bayes\' theorem: the share of the evidence that came through each explanation.' },
          { idea: 'Conditioning on the first step is the most useful move in interview probability. "What happens first?" splits a hard problem into cases, each easier than the whole. The recursion chapter builds on exactly this.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Bayes with natural frequencies', blocks: [
          `<p>Bayes' theorem says P(H | E) = P(E | H)P(H)/P(E). In practice, imagine a crowd and count. A rare condition plus an imperfect test gives many more false positives than true ones, so a positive result is less conclusive than it feels.</p>
           <p>Where the formula comes from: P(H and E) can be written two ways, P(H)P(E | H) or P(E)P(H | E). Set them equal and divide by P(E). The denominator P(E) is the law of total probability: P(E | H)P(H) + P(E | not H)P(not H). So Bayes is just "the share of the evidence that came through H", the same calculation as the urn above.</p>
           <p><b>Natural frequencies</b> make this hard to get wrong: pick a round population (1,000 or 10,000), split it by the prior, then by the test, and count the people who test positive.</p>`,
          { fig: 'dots', cols: 50, size: 7.6, groups: [{ n: 8, tone: 'ok', t: '8 have it and test positive' }, { n: 2, tone: 'bad', t: '2 have it and are missed' }, { n: 50, tone: 'acc', t: '50 healthy, but flagged' }, { n: 940, t: '940 healthy and clear' }], alt: 'One thousand dots. Only 8 are true positives, against 50 false alarms.', cap: '1,000 people. One in 100 has it, the test catches 80%, and 5% of healthy people are flagged. Of 58 positives, only 8 are real: about 14%.' },
          { widget: 'bayes' },
          { q: '2% of people have a condition. The test catches 90% of cases and wrongly flags 10% of healthy people. Someone tests positive. What is the probability they have it?', a: 0.018 / (0.018 + 0.098), tol: { abs: 0.003 }, hint: 'Out of 1,000 people, how many are true positives and how many false positives?', sol: 'Take 1,000 people. 20 have the condition, and the test catches 90% of them: 18 true positives. 980 don\'t, and 10% of those are flagged: 98 false positives. Of the 116 positives, 18 are real: 18/116 ≈ <b>0.155</b>. The false positives win because the healthy group is 49 times bigger.' },
          { market: 'Base rates decide how you read a signal. If only 1 in 50 ideas has real edge, a backtest that "passes" 5% of bad ideas and 80% of good ones leaves about 3 false discoveries for each real one.' },
          `<p>The fix for a weak test is more independent evidence. A second independent positive in the example above multiplies the odds again: the first positive takes the odds from 2 : 98 to 18 : 98, and a second takes them to 162 : 98, a probability of about 62%. Retesting is how doctors, and traders confirming a signal on new data, deal with base rates.</p>`,
        ] },
        { h: 'Bayes in odds form', blocks: [
          `<p>Posterior odds = prior odds × likelihood ratio, where the likelihood ratio is P(evidence | H)/P(evidence | not H). It's the fastest form in interviews, and repeated independent evidence simply multiplies.</p>
           <p>Why it works: write Bayes for H and for "not H" and divide one by the other. P(E) cancels, which is the messy part, and you're left with posterior odds = prior odds × likelihood ratio. Converting back: odds a : b mean probability a/(a + b).</p>
           <p>The likelihood ratio measures how <b>diagnostic</b> the evidence is. A ratio of 1 means the evidence is equally likely either way, and changes nothing, however striking it looks. A ratio of 10 moves you a lot. Taking logs turns the multiplication into addition: each piece of independent evidence adds its log likelihood ratio to your running total. That's the mathematical form of "weighing the evidence".</p>`,
          { q: 'Prior odds of a coin being biased are 1 : 4. A flip comes up heads, which is 3 times as likely if it is biased. What is the probability it is biased now?', a: 3 / 7, hint: 'Multiply the odds by 3, then convert odds a : b to a/(a + b).', sol: 'Prior odds 1 : 4, likelihood ratio 3: posterior odds 3 : 4. Converting odds to a probability: 3/(3 + 4) = <b>3/7</b> ≈ 0.43. Up from the prior 1/5 = 0.2.' },
          { id: 'a1', q: 'Same coin, same prior of 1 : 4. Now two independent heads come up. What is the probability it is biased?', a: 9 / 13, tol: { abs: 0.003 }, hint: 'Multiply by the likelihood ratio twice.', sol: 'Independent evidence multiplies: 1 : 4 × 3 × 3 = 9 : 4, so P = 9/13 ≈ <b>0.69</b>. Each head adds the same log-odds; after three heads the odds would be 27 : 4.' },
          { worked: 'which coin?', q: 'A bag has 9 fair coins and 1 coin with heads on both sides. You draw one and flip it 5 times: all heads. What is the probability it is the two-headed coin?', steps: [
            ['Prior odds of two-headed : fair?', '1 : 9.'],
            ['Likelihood ratio of one head?', 'P(H | two-headed)/P(H | fair) = 1/(½) = 2.'],
            ['Five independent heads?', 'Ratio 2⁵ = 32.'],
            'Posterior odds 32 : 9, so P = 32/41 ≈ <b>0.78</b>.',
            ['One tail appears. Now what?', 'The two-headed coin can\'t show tails: likelihood ratio 0, so P = 0. One decisive observation beats any number of weak ones.'],
          ] },
        ] },
        { h: 'Monty Hall, properly', blocks: [
          { worked: 'the clearest proof', q: 'You pick door 1 of 3. The host, who knows where the car is, always opens a door with a goat that you didn\'t pick. Here it\'s door 3. Should you switch?', steps: [
            ['Prior: where is the car?', 'Each door has 1/3.'],
            ['How likely is "host opens 3" if the car is behind door 1?', '½, because he can open 2 or 3.'],
            ['And if the car is behind door 2?', '1, because he must open door 3.'],
            ['And if it\'s behind door 3?', '0, because he never reveals the car.'],
            'So the evidence has likelihoods ½ : 1 : 0, and the posterior is ⅓ × ½ : ⅓ × 1 : 0 = 1 : 2 : 0. Door 2 has probability 2/3. <b>Switch.</b>',
            ['What would change if the host opened a door at random?', 'Then "opens 3 and shows a goat" has likelihood ½ if the car is behind door 1 or door 2, and it\'s 50–50. The answer depends on the host\'s rule, not only on what you saw.'],
          ] },
          { idea: 'Evidence isn\'t just what you saw. It\'s how likely you were to see it under each story. That\'s also how to read a trade: ask who would have done this, and why.' },
          { id: 'm1', q: 'A family has two children and you learn at least one is a girl. What is P(two girls)?', opts: [['1/3'], ['1/2', 'List GG, GB, BG, BB. The condition removes only BB.'], ['1/4', 'That\'s before you had any information.'], ['2/3', 'That\'s the chance of a boy-and-girl family.']], sol: 'The equally likely families are GG, GB, BG and BB. "At least one girl" removes only BB, leaving three, of which one is GG: 1/3. If you\'d met one child at random and she was a girl, the answer would be 1/2, because a GG family is twice as likely to show you a girl as a GB family. How you learned it matters.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Independence and conditional independence', blocks: [
          `<p>Two events can be independent <b>given</b> some hidden fact, yet dependent when you don't know it. Flips of one coin are independent given the coin's bias, but if the bias is unknown, each head is evidence about the bias, and so makes the next head more likely.</p>
           <p>This is how learning works, and it matters in markets: returns on two stocks may be nearly independent given the market's move, but strongly related when you don't know it, because both depend on it. Calling things "independent" without saying what you're conditioning on is a common source of error.</p>`,
          { worked: 'a coin you don\'t know', q: 'A bag holds one fair coin and one two-headed coin. You pick one at random and flip it twice. Given the first flip is heads, what is the probability the second is heads?', steps: [
            ['P(first flip heads)?', 'Average over the coins: ½ × ½ + ½ × 1 = 3/4.'],
            ['P(both heads)?', '½ × ¼ + ½ × 1 = 5/8.'],
            ['So P(second H | first H)?', '(5/8)/(3/4) = <b>5/6</b>.'],
            'That\'s more than the unconditional 3/4: the first head made the two-headed coin more likely (it\'s now 2/3), which raises the chance of the next head. Given the coin, the flips are independent; without knowing it, they are not.',
          ] },
          { id: 'x2', q: 'Same bag (one fair coin, one two-headed). You flip your chosen coin three times and see three heads. What is the probability the next flip is heads? (Give a decimal.)', a: (0.5 * 0.5 ** 4 + 0.5) / (0.5 * 0.5 ** 3 + 0.5), tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'First find P(two-headed | three heads) with odds, then average the next-flip chances.', sol: 'Odds two-headed : fair start at 1 : 1 and three heads multiply them by 2³ = 8: P(two-headed) = 8/9. Next flip: 8/9 × 1 + 1/9 × ½ = 17/18 ≈ <b>0.944</b>.' },
        ] },
      ],
      takeaways: ['Condition by restricting to the outcomes where the evidence happened, then rescale.', 'P(A | B) ≠ P(B | A).', 'Trees multiply along branches (the chain rule); the law of total probability adds the branches.', 'Natural frequencies make Bayes intuitive; odds × likelihood ratio makes it fast, and independent evidence multiplies.', 'Rare events + imperfect tests ⇒ most positives are false. More independent evidence is the cure.', 'Always ask how likely the evidence was under each story, including how it came to be shown to you.', 'Independent given a hidden fact is not independent.'],
      explain: { q: 'Explain to a friend why a positive test for a rare disease can still mean you probably don\'t have it.', a: 'Imagine 1,000 people. A few have the disease, and the test finds most of them. But the healthy group is so much larger that even a small false-alarm rate produces more positives than the real cases. So most positives come from healthy people. A strong answer adds what would change it: a second independent positive test, or a higher starting chance (symptoms, exposure), makes a real case much more likely.' },
      homework: [
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'Two dice. Given that the first shows at least 4, what is the probability that the total is at least 9?', a: 1 / 2, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'Only 18 outcomes remain. Count totals of 9 or more among them.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> shrink the world to the outcomes where the first die is 4, 5 or 6 (3 × 6 = 18 equally likely pairs), then count.</p><ol><li>First = 4: total ≥ 9 needs the second to be 5 or 6 (2 ways).</li><li>First = 5: second 4, 5 or 6 (3 ways).</li><li>First = 6: second 3 to 6 (4 ways).</li><li>9 of 18: <b>1/2</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Check:</b> unconditionally P(total ≥ 9) = 10/36 ≈ 0.28. Knowing the first die is high should raise it, and it does.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'A deck has 3 cards: red/red, red/black, black/black. You draw a card and see a red side. What is the probability that the other side is red?', a: 2 / 3, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'Count red sides, not cards.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> the equally likely outcomes are the six card <i>sides</i> you could be looking at, not the three cards.</p><ol><li>The red sides: two on the red/red card, one on the red/black card. You are looking at one of these three, equally likely.</li><li>In 2 of the 3 cases the other side is red: <b>2/3</b>.</li></ol><p><b>The trap</b> is "it\'s red/red or red/black, so ½". That treats the cards as equally likely given what you saw, but red/red is twice as likely to show you red. In odds: prior 1 : 1, likelihood ratio P(red side | RR)/P(red side | RB) = 1/(½) = 2, posterior 2 : 1.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'A factory has machine A (60% of output, 1% faulty) and machine B (40% of output, 3% faulty). What fraction of all items are faulty?', a: 0.018, hint: 'Total probability.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> law of total probability, weighting each machine\'s fault rate by its share of output.</p><ol><li>From A: 0.6 × 0.01 = 0.006.</li><li>From B: 0.4 × 0.03 = 0.012.</li><li>Total: 0.006 + 0.012 = <b>0.018</b>, or 1.8%.</li></ol><p><b>Check:</b> the answer must lie between the two machines\' rates (1% and 3%), nearer A\'s because A makes more. 1.8% does.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'In that factory, an item is faulty. What is the probability it came from machine B?', a: 2 / 3, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: '0.012/0.018.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> Bayes: the share of all faulty items that come from B.</p><ol><li>Faulty items from B: 0.4 × 0.03 = 0.012 of all output (previous question).</li><li>All faulty items: 0.018.</li><li>P(B | faulty) = 0.012/0.018 = <b>2/3</b>.</li></ol><p><b>In odds:</b> prior A : B = 60 : 40 = 3 : 2, likelihood ratio of a fault (B over A) = 3, so posterior B : A = 2 × 3 : 3 = 6 : 3 = 2 : 1. B makes less but is three times as faulty, so it accounts for most faults.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'One of 100 coins is double-headed. You pick one at random and flip it 7 times, getting 7 heads. What is the probability you have the double-headed coin?', a: 128 / 227, tol: { abs: 0.003 }, hint: 'Prior odds 1 : 99, likelihood ratio 2⁷.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> odds form of Bayes, because the flips are independent given the coin.</p><ol><li>Prior odds double-headed : fair = 1 : 99.</li><li>Each head is certain with the double-headed coin and has chance ½ with a fair one: likelihood ratio 2 per head, 2⁷ = 128 for seven.</li><li>Posterior odds 128 : 99, so P = 128/227 ≈ <b>0.564</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Sense check:</b> the prior is tiny (1%), but 7 heads is a 1-in-128 event for a fair coin, so the evidence just about overturns it. With 10 heads the odds would be 1024 : 99, about 91%.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'You think a counterparty is informed with probability 0.2. If informed, they buy 90% of the time. If not, 50%. They buy. What is the probability that they are informed?', a: 0.18 / (0.18 + 0.4), tol: { abs: 0.003 }, hint: 'Odds 1 : 4, likelihood ratio 0.9/0.5.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> Bayes, in odds or in frequencies.</p><ol><li>Frequencies: of 100 counterparties, 20 are informed and 18 of them buy; 80 are not and 40 of them buy.</li><li>Of 58 buyers, 18 are informed: 18/58 ≈ <b>0.310</b>.</li><li>Odds check: prior 1 : 4, likelihood ratio 0.9/0.5 = 1.8, posterior 1.8 : 4, so P = 1.8/5.8 ≈ 0.310.</li></ol><p><b>Why it matters:</b> this is a market maker\'s update. A buy is evidence the buyer knows something, so you should raise your estimate of value after being bought from. That\'s adverse selection, and it\'s why spreads exist.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'stretch', q: 'Two people each roll a die in secret and tell you only "at least one of us rolled a six". What is P(both rolled a six)? Now suppose that instead you ask one of them at random and they say "I rolled a six". What is it then? Why do they differ?', sol: '<p><b>First case:</b> "at least one six" leaves the 11 pairs that contain a 6 (6 with the first die, 6 with the second, minus the double counted (6,6)). Only (6,6) is a double six: <b>1/11</b>.</p><p><b>Second case:</b> you learn that one specific die is a 6. The other die is independent of it: <b>1/6</b>.</p><p><b>Why they differ:</b> the statements carry different information. "At least one" only rules out the 25 pairs with no six. Naming a particular die rules out everything where that die isn\'t a 6, 30 pairs, leaving (6,1)…(6,6). Same principle as the two-children puzzle: what you learn depends on how the information was produced, not just on its words.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'lab', q: 'Play Monty Hall 30 times with three cups and a coin, always switching. Tally your wins. Then think about what you\'d expect with 100 doors, where the host opens 98.', sol: '<p>You should win about 20 of 30 (two-thirds), give or take 3 or so from chance.</p><p>With 100 doors: your first pick is right 1% of the time. The host, who knows where the car is, opens 98 of the other 99 doors, all goats. If your first pick was wrong (99%), the car must be behind the one door he left closed. So switching wins 99% of the time. It\'s the same logic as three doors, just obvious, and it\'s the best way to explain the puzzle to someone who doesn\'t believe it.</p>' },
      ],
      practice: [['Bayes topic', '#/topic/bayes'], ['Monty Hall', '#/iq/cl-monty-hall'], ['Two-child problem', '#/iq/imc-two-child'], ['Bayes interview questions', '#/search/bayes']],
    },

    expectation: {
      title: 'Random variables and expected value', minutes: 50,
      why: 'Expected value is the price of anything uncertain. A trader who can\'t compute it can\'t trade. Linearity of expectation is also the most powerful shortcut in interview maths.',
      goals: ['Compute expected values and price simple games', 'Use linearity of expectation, even for dependent variables', 'Count with indicator variables', 'Condition on cases with the law of total expectation', 'Use the tail-sum formula for maxima and waiting times', 'Avoid the E[g(X)] = g(E[X]) trap'],
      prereq: ['prob-basics', 'conditional'],
      sections: [
        { h: 'Expected value', blocks: [
          `<p>A <b>random variable</b> X is a number that depends on chance. Its expected value is the probability-weighted average of its values:</p>
           <div class="hb-eq">E[X] = Σ x · P(X = x)</div>
           <p>It's the long-run average per play, which is why it's the fair price of a game paid out as X. It needn't be a value X can take: a die has E = 3.5.</p>
           <p>Two pictures help. <b>Long run:</b> play a million times and the average payout per play settles near E[X] (the law of large numbers, below). <b>Balance point:</b> put a weight P(X = x) at each value x on a ruler; E[X] is where the ruler balances. A small weight far out moves the balance point a lot, which is why rare, large outcomes dominate expected values.</p>
           <p>That second picture is a warning. A lottery ticket with a 1-in-10-million chance of £20 million is "worth" £2, yet almost every ticket pays nothing. The mean tells you the fair price of a repeated bet; it says nothing about what usually happens. Always ask what the distribution looks like, not just its average.</p>`,
          { widget: 'lln' },
          { q: 'What is the expected value of a fair die roll?', a: 3.5, hint: 'Average the faces.', sol: 'Each face has weight 1/6: (1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 + 6)/6 = 21/6 = <b>3.5</b>. It\'s the midpoint of 1 and 6, as it must be for a symmetric distribution.' },
          { worked: 'a lopsided game', q: 'A game pays $10 with probability 0.1, $2 with probability 0.3 and nothing otherwise. What is it worth, and what usually happens?', steps: [
            ['Weight each payout by its probability.', '10 × 0.1 + 2 × 0.3 + 0 × 0.6 = 1 + 0.6 + 0 = 1.6.'],
            'So E = <b>$1.60</b>: the fair price for playing it many times.',
            ['What is the most common outcome?', '$0, 60% of the time. The rare $10 supplies most of the value.'],
          ] },
        ] },
        { h: 'Linearity of expectation', blocks: [
          `<p>E[X + Y] = E[X] + E[Y] and E[aX] = aE[X], <b>always</b>, even when X and Y are dependent. This is the most useful fact in interview probability: break a complicated total into simple pieces and add their expectations.</p>
           <p>Why it's so powerful: the distribution of a sum is usually hard (it needs a convolution, or the joint distribution). Its expectation is free. Most "expected number of …" interview questions are designed so that the distribution is a nightmare and linearity is one line.</p>`,
          { proof: 'For discrete variables: E[X + Y] = Σ<sub>x,y</sub> (x + y) P(x, y) = Σ x P(x, y) + Σ y P(x, y). Summing over y in the first term gives Σ x P(x) = E[X], and likewise E[Y] in the second. We never used independence.' },
          { q: 'What is the expected total of three dice?', a: 10.5, hint: 'Add three expectations.', sol: 'Each die contributes 3.5 on average, and expectations add: 3 × 3.5 = <b>10.5</b>. No need for the distribution of the total (which takes 216 outcomes to write down).' },
          { trap: 'Linearity is for sums. E[XY] = E[X]E[Y] needs independence (more precisely, zero covariance), and E[X/Y] is almost never E[X]/E[Y].' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Indicator variables', blocks: [
          `<p>To find the expected <i>number</i> of things that happen, write the count as a sum of indicators: I<sub>i</sub> is 1 if thing i happens, otherwise 0. Each indicator's expectation is just its probability. You never need the full distribution.</p>
           <p>The recipe: (1) say what you are counting; (2) list the places it could happen (positions, people, pairs); (3) find the probability it happens at one place, usually the same everywhere by symmetry; (4) multiply by the number of places. The indicators can be as dependent as they like.</p>`,
          { fig: 'strip', cell: 30, labelW: 72, rows: [{ label: 'flips', cells: ['H', 'T', 'H', 'H', 'T', 'H', 'H', 'H'] }, { label: 'HH here?', cells: [0, 0, { t: 1, tone: 'acc' }, 0, 0, { t: 1, tone: 'acc' }, { t: 1, tone: 'acc' }, { gap: true }] }], alt: 'Eight coin flips with an indicator below each position marking where HH starts.', cap: 'Number of HH pairs = sum of indicators. There are 7 positions, each 1 with probability ¼, so E = 7/4, even though neighbouring indicators are dependent.' },
          { q: 'You roll a die 6 times. What is the expected number of different faces you see?', a: 6 * (1 - (5 / 6) ** 6), tol: { abs: 0.01 }, hint: 'For each face, what is the chance it appears at least once?', sol: 'Use an indicator for each face: is it seen at least once? A face is missed by all six rolls with probability (5/6)⁶ ≈ 0.335, so it appears with probability ≈ 0.665. Six faces: 6 × 0.665 ≈ <b>3.99</b>. Fewer than you might guess: in six rolls you usually see about four different faces.' },
          { q: '10 people take random hats from a pile of their own hats. How many expect to get their own hat?', a: 1, hint: 'Each person gets their own hat with what probability?', sol: 'Indicator per person: they get their own hat with probability 1/10 (any hat is equally likely to be theirs). Ten people: 10 × 1/10 = <b>1</b>. The same for any number of people, since n × 1/n = 1, even though whether one person gets their hat affects the others.' },
          { worked: 'runs', q: 'You flip a coin 10 times. A run is a maximal block of equal flips (HHTHTT has 4 runs). What is the expected number of runs?', steps: [
            ['Where does a run start?', 'At the first flip, and at every flip that differs from the one before it.'],
            ['So the count is 1 plus the number of "changes". How many places can a change happen, and how likely is each?', '9 boundaries between neighbouring flips, each a change with probability ½.'],
            'E[runs] = 1 + 9 × ½ = <b>5.5</b>.',
          ] },
          { worked: 'coupon collector', q: 'A cereal box holds one of 6 toys at random. How many boxes do you expect to buy to collect all 6?', steps: [
            ['Split the wait into stages. What is the wait for the first new toy?', 'Exactly 1 box.'],
            ['With 1 toy owned, what is the chance a box brings a new one?', '5/6, so on average you wait 6/5 boxes.'],
            ['And with k toys owned?', '(6 − k)/6, so the wait is 6/(6 − k) boxes.'],
            'Linearity adds the stage waits: 6(1/6 + 1/5 + 1/4 + 1/3 + 1/2 + 1) = 14.7 boxes.',
          ] },
          { id: 'a1', q: 'How many rolls of a die do you expect to need to see all six faces? Same maths, but do it yourself.', a: 14.7, tol: { abs: 0.01 }, hint: '6 × (1 + ½ + ⅓ + ¼ + ⅕ + ⅙).', sol: 'Stage k (from k − 1 faces seen to k) succeeds with probability (7 − k)/6, so it takes 6/(7 − k) rolls on average. Adding the stages: 6 × (1 + ½ + ⅓ + ¼ + ⅕ + ⅙) = 6 × 2.45 = <b>14.7</b>. The last face alone takes 6 rolls on average: the end of a collection is the slow part.' },
          { id: 'x1', q: 'You flip a coin 20 times. What is the expected number of runs?', a: 10.5, tol: { abs: 0.01 }, hint: '1 plus the expected number of changes between neighbours.', sol: '19 boundaries, each a change with probability ½: 1 + 19/2 = <b>10.5</b>.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Conditioning: the law of total expectation', blocks: [
          `<p>Just as probabilities can be split by cases, so can expectations:</p>
           <div class="hb-eq">E[X] = Σ E[X | case] · P(case)</div>
           <p>Choose cases that make each piece easy, often "what happens first". This is how you find waiting times. Flip a coin until the first head: with probability ½ the first flip is a head and you're done (1 flip); otherwise you've used one flip and are back where you started. So E = ½ × 1 + ½ × (1 + E), giving E = 2.</p>
           <p>The same argument for any success probability p gives E = 1/p: the expected wait for a 1-in-p event is p tries. A six takes 6 rolls on average; a 5 or 6 takes 3.</p>`,
          { id: 'x2', q: 'You roll a die until it shows a 5 or a 6. What is the expected number of rolls?', a: 3, hint: 'Each roll succeeds with probability 1/3.', sol: 'Each roll succeeds with probability 2/6 = 1/3. First step: E = ⅓ × 1 + ⅔ × (1 + E), so ⅓E = 1 and E = <b>3</b>, which is 1/p.' },
          { worked: 'a game that depends on the first roll', q: 'Roll a die. If it shows 1–3 you get that many dollars. If it shows 4–6 you roll again and get the second roll in dollars. What is the game worth?', steps: [
            ['Case 1–3 (probability ½): what is the average payout?', '(1 + 2 + 3)/3 = 2.'],
            ['Case 4–6 (probability ½): what is the average payout?', 'A fresh roll: 3.5.'],
            'E = ½ × 2 + ½ × 3.5 = <b>2.75</b>.',
          ] },
        ] },
        { h: 'The tail-sum formula', blocks: [
          `<p>For a variable taking values 0, 1, 2, …, there's a second way to compute the mean:</p>
           <div class="hb-eq">E[X] = Σ<sub>k≥1</sub> P(X ≥ k)</div>
           <p>Why: X itself is a sum of indicators, X = Σ<sub>k≥1</sub> 1(X ≥ k). (A value of 3 switches on the indicators for k = 1, 2 and 3.) Take expectations of both sides. This is the best tool for <b>maxima</b> and <b>waiting times</b>, because P(max ≥ k) and P(wait ≥ k) are easy when P(max = k) isn't.</p>`,
          { worked: 'the larger of two dice, via tails', q: 'What is the expected value of the larger of two dice?', steps: [
            ['P(max ≥ k) is awkward; its complement is easy. What is P(max < k)?', 'Both dice must be below k: ((k − 1)/6)².'],
            'So P(max ≥ k) = 1 − (k − 1)²/36, for k = 1 to 6.',
            'Add: Σ 1 − (0 + 1 + 4 + 9 + 16 + 25)/36 = 6 − 55/36 = 161/36 ≈ <b>4.47</b>.',
            'Larger than 3.5, as the maximum of two must be. The homework does the same question by the direct route.',
          ] },
        ] },
        { h: 'Pricing a game', blocks: [
          `<p>A game's value is E[payout] − cost. Positive means you'd play at that price; the fair price makes it zero.</p>
           <p>Casino games are priced so that the house has a small, reliable edge. European roulette has 37 pockets and pays 35 to 1 on a single number: per $1 bet, E = (35 × 1 − 1 × 36)/37 = −1/37 ≈ −2.7%. No betting system changes that, because any sequence of bets is a sum of individually negative bets, and linearity adds them up.</p>
           <p>Market makers live the same way from the other side: a small positive edge on each of a very large number of trades. The edge per trade must cover costs and risk; the volume turns it into a business.</p>`,
          { q: 'You pay $1 to roll a die and receive $5 if it shows a six. What is your expected profit?', a: -1 / 6, tol: { abs: 0.005 }, hint: 'Expected winnings minus the cost.', sol: 'Expected winnings: 5 × 1/6 = 5/6 ≈ $0.83. Minus the $1 you pay: 5/6 − 1 = <b>−1/6</b> ≈ −$0.17 a game. The fair prize would be $6, so that the six pays for the five games you lose.' },
          { id: 'x3', q: 'European roulette: 37 pockets, a single-number bet pays 35 to 1 (you get your $1 back plus $35). What is your expected profit per $1 bet? (Give a decimal.)', a: -1 / 37, tol: { abs: 0.0005 }, hint: 'Win 35 with probability 1/37; lose 1 otherwise.', sol: '35 × 1/37 − 1 × 36/37 = −1/37 ≈ <b>−0.027</b>. A fair payout would be 36 to 1; the house keeps the difference.' },
          { id: 'm1', q: 'A game is worth $100 on average, but pays $0 with probability 99% and $10,000 with probability 1%. What should you pay to play it once?', opts: [['Less than $100, for most people'], ['Exactly $100: that\'s its value', 'That\'s the fair price if you can repeat it many times. Once, you also carry risk.'], ['Nothing: you will almost surely lose', 'You\'d take it for $1.'], ['$10,000: that\'s the prize', 'You only win it 1% of the time.']], sol: 'Expected value is the price of a repeatable bet. For a one-off bet that\'s large for you, risk also matters: you\'ll almost certainly lose your stake. The betting chapter makes this precise.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'E[g(X)] is not g(E[X])', blocks: [
          `<p>The expected square isn't the square of the expectation. For a convex g (a curve that bends upward, like x²), E[g(X)] ≥ g(E[X]). This is <b>Jensen's inequality</b>.</p>`,
          { fig: 'plot', x: [0, 7], y: [0, 40], xt: [1, 3.5, 6], yt: [0, 12.25, 18.5], fns: [{ f: (x) => x * x }], segs: [[1, 1, 6, 36, 'muted'], [3.5, 0, 3.5, 18.5, 'muted']], pts: [{ x: 3.5, y: 12.25, tone: 'ok' }, { x: 3.5, y: 18.5, tone: 'bad' }, { x: 1, y: 1, tone: 'acc' }, { x: 6, y: 36, tone: 'acc' }], labels: [{ x: 3.7, y: 10.2, t: 'g(average)', tone: 'ok' }, { x: 3.7, y: 19.8, t: 'average of g', tone: 'bad' }], alt: 'The curve x squared with a chord between x equals 1 and 6. The chord lies above the curve.', cap: 'For two equally likely outcomes, 1 and 6, the average of g sits on the chord, above the curve. Convexity makes variability valuable: it\'s why options have value.' },
          `<p>Where it bites in practice:</p>
           <ul><li><b>Options.</b> A call pays max(S − K, 0), a convex function of the price. Its expected payoff is more than the payoff at the expected price, which is why an option has value even when it's at the money.</li>
           <li><b>Average speed.</b> Drive to work at 30 mph and back at 60 mph: the average speed is 40 mph, not 45. Time is 1/speed, a convex function, so averaging speeds gets it wrong.</li>
           <li><b>Compounding.</b> Up 50% then down 50% leaves you at 75%, not 100%. The log is concave, so E[log return] is below log of the average return: volatility drags on growth.</li></ul>`,
          { q: 'You are paid the square of a die roll. What is the game worth?', a: 91 / 6, hint: 'Average 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36.', sol: 'E[X²] = (1 + 4 + 9 + 16 + 25 + 36)/6 = 91/6 ≈ <b>15.17</b>, more than (E[X])² = 3.5² = 12.25. The gap, 91/6 − 49/4 = 35/12 ≈ 2.92, is exactly the variance of a die: that\'s the next chapter.' },
        ] },
      ],
      takeaways: ['E[X] is the long-run average: the fair price of a repeated bet. It isn\'t what usually happens.', 'Linearity always holds, dependence or not. E[XY] and E[X/Y] don\'t split.', 'Counts = sums of indicators; E[indicator] = probability.', 'Waiting for a 1-in-p event takes p tries; for several things, split into stages and add.', 'Condition on the first step: E[X] = Σ E[X | case]P(case).', 'Maxima and waits: E[X] = Σ P(X ≥ k).', 'E[g(X)] ≠ g(E[X]) unless g is linear; convex payoffs like variability.'],
      explain: { q: 'Why does linearity of expectation work even for dependent variables, when variances don\'t simply add?', a: 'An expectation is a weighted average, and summing over one variable makes the joint probabilities collapse to each variable\'s own distribution, with no need for independence. Variance involves the square of a sum, which creates cross terms: those are the covariances, and they depend on how the variables move together.' },
      homework: [
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'What is the expected value of the larger of two dice?', a: 161 / 36, tol: { abs: 0.01 }, hint: 'P(max = k) = (2k − 1)/36.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> find the distribution of the maximum, then average.</p><ol><li>P(max ≤ k) = (k/6)², since both dice must be at most k. So P(max = k) = (k² − (k − 1)²)/36 = (2k − 1)/36: 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11 thirty-sixths for k = 1…6.</li><li>E = (1·1 + 2·3 + 3·5 + 4·7 + 5·9 + 6·11)/36 = (1 + 6 + 15 + 28 + 45 + 66)/36 = 161/36.</li><li>≈ <b>4.47</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Check:</b> the tail-sum route (in the chapter) gives 6 − 55/36 = 161/36 too. And the minimum is 7 − 4.47 = 2.53, because max + min = sum of the dice, whose mean is 7.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'A coin is flipped 20 times. What is the expected number of times that heads is immediately followed by tails?', a: 19 / 4, tol: { abs: 0.01 }, hint: '19 adjacent pairs, each HT with probability ¼.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> indicators, one per adjacent pair.</p><ol><li>There are 19 adjacent pairs (positions 1–2, 2–3, …, 19–20).</li><li>Each pair is H then T with probability ½ × ½ = ¼.</li><li>E = 19 × ¼ = 19/4 = <b>4.75</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Note:</b> neighbouring pairs are dependent (an HT at positions 3–4 rules one out at 4–5), but linearity doesn\'t need independence.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'From a shuffled deck, you deal 5 cards. What is the expected number of aces?', a: 5 / 13, tol: { abs: 0.003 }, hint: 'Each card is an ace with probability 1/13.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> one indicator per card dealt.</p><ol><li>By symmetry each of the 5 positions is an ace with probability 4/52 = 1/13.</li><li>E = 5 × 1/13 = <b>5/13</b> ≈ 0.385.</li></ol><p><b>Another way:</b> one indicator per ace: each ace is among your 5 cards with probability 5/52. Four aces: 20/52 = 5/13. Choosing what to put the indicators on is part of the skill.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'A game pays you $X, where X is the roll of a die, and you pay $4 for it. What is your expected profit?', a: -0.5, hint: '3.5 − 4.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> value = E[payout] − cost.</p><ol><li>E[payout] = E[die] = $3.50.</li><li>Profit = 3.5 − 4 = <b>−$0.50</b> per game.</li></ol><p><b>Interpretation:</b> the fair price is $3.50. At $4 you would sell the game, not buy it: whoever offers it at $4 has a 50-cent edge per game.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: '5 people each pick a random other person among the 5 to pass a ball to (not themselves). What is the expected number of people who receive no ball?', a: 5 * (3 / 4) ** 4, tol: { abs: 0.01 }, hint: 'Take one person. Four others could pick them, each with probability ¼.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> one indicator per person: do they receive no ball?</p><ol><li>Fix a person. Each of the other 4 picks among 4 people (everyone but themselves), so picks our person with probability ¼ and misses with probability ¾.</li><li>The others choose independently, so P(nobody picks them) = (3/4)⁴ = 81/256 ≈ 0.316.</li><li>Five people: 5 × 81/256 = 405/256 ≈ <b>1.58</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Check:</b> 5 balls go to 5 people, so at least one person usually gets two and someone gets none. An average of about 1.6 empty-handed is reasonable.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'You draw from a deck one card at a time without replacement until you draw the first ace. What is the expected number of cards you draw?', a: 53 / 5, tol: { abs: 0.01 }, hint: 'The 4 aces split the 48 others into 5 equal gaps on average.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> count the non-aces before the first ace with indicators, then add 1 for the ace itself.</p><ol><li>Take one non-ace card. It comes before all 4 aces exactly when it is first among itself and the 4 aces, which by symmetry has probability 1/5.</li><li>48 non-aces: expected number before the first ace = 48/5 = 9.6.</li><li>Add the ace: 9.6 + 1 = 53/5 = <b>10.6</b>.</li></ol><p><b>The picture:</b> the 4 aces cut the 48 other cards into 5 gaps, and by symmetry each gap holds 48/5 cards on average. You\'ll see this argument again in the symmetry chapter.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'stretch', q: 'Let X be non-negative with E[X] = 2. Show that P(X ≥ 10) ≤ 0.2 for any such X, and explain what this says about how fat a tail can be.', sol: '<p><b>Proof:</b> split the expectation on whether X ≥ 10.</p><ol><li>E[X] = E[X; X ≥ 10] + E[X; X < 10].</li><li>On the event X ≥ 10, X is at least 10, so the first term is at least 10 × P(X ≥ 10). The second term is at least 0, because X ≥ 0.</li><li>So 2 = E[X] ≥ 10 × P(X ≥ 10), giving P(X ≥ 10) ≤ 0.2.</li></ol><p>This is <b>Markov\'s inequality</b>: P(X ≥ a) ≤ E[X]/a for non-negative X. It\'s tight: X = 10 with probability 0.2 and 0 otherwise has mean 2 and achieves it. So a mean alone limits how much probability can sit far out, but only weakly. Knowing the variance as well gives much sharper limits (Chebyshev, next chapter).</p>' },
        { lvl: 'lab', q: 'Open a pack of cards. Deal until you see the first ace, and record how many cards it took. Repeat 15 times and average. How close are you to 10.6?', sol: '<p>The number of cards has a standard deviation of about 8.2 (it is skewed: often small, occasionally 30+), so the average of 15 trials has a standard error of about 8.2/√15 ≈ 2.1. Anything from about 6.5 to 15 is ordinary.</p><p><b>Why it matters:</b> 15 trials can\'t pin down a mean precisely. The same is true of judging a trading strategy from a handful of trades, which is what Part 3 (statistics) is about.</p>' },
      ],
      practice: [['Expected value topic', '#/topic/ev'], ['Expected value interview questions', '#/search/expected%20value'], ['Reroll game', '#/iq/js-reroll']],
    },

    variance: {
      title: 'Variance, covariance and correlation', minutes: 50,
      why: 'Expected value says where you should end up; variance says how bumpy the ride is. In trading, risk is variance, and diversification is covariance. Everything about portfolios rests on this chapter.',
      goals: ['Compute variance and standard deviation, and bound tails with Chebyshev', 'Use the rules for scaling and adding random variables', 'Find the variance of a count of dependent indicators', 'Understand covariance, correlation and the √n rule', 'Compute the risk of a two-asset portfolio and its minimum-variance mix'],
      prereq: ['expectation'],
      sections: [
        { h: 'Variance measures spread', blocks: [
          `<p>Var(X) = E[(X − μ)²] = E[X²] − (E[X])². It's the average squared distance from the mean. The standard deviation σ = √Var is in the same units as X, so it's the number you can actually picture.</p>
           <p>Why square the distances, rather than average |X − μ|? Squares make the algebra work: variances of independent variables add, which absolute deviations don't. They also punish big misses more than small ones, which suits risk: one 10% loss hurts more than ten 1% losses.</p>
           <p>A useful special case: a 0–1 variable (an indicator) with P(1) = p has E[X²] = E[X] = p, so Var = p − p² = <b>p(1 − p)</b>. It's largest at p = ½: a coin flip is the most uncertain yes/no.</p>`,
          { fig: 'bars', vals: [9, 4, 1, 1, 4, 9].map((x) => x / 4), labs: ['1', '2', '3', '4', '5', '6'], show: ['6.25', '2.25', '0.25', '0.25', '2.25', '6.25'], w: 320, h: 180, tone: () => 'acc-soft', xl: 'face of the die', alt: 'Squared distance of each die face from 3.5: 6.25, 2.25, 0.25, 0.25, 2.25, 6.25.', cap: 'Squared distance of each face from the mean of 3.5. Their average is the variance.' },
          { proof: 'Expand: E[(X − μ)²] = E[X²] − 2μE[X] + μ² = E[X²] − 2μ² + μ² = E[X²] − μ².' },
          { q: 'What is the variance of a fair die roll?', a: 35 / 12, tol: { abs: 0.005 }, hint: 'E[X²] − (E[X])².', sol: 'E[X²] = (1 + 4 + 9 + 16 + 25 + 36)/6 = 91/6, and (E[X])² = 3.5² = 49/4. Var = 91/6 − 49/4 = 182/12 − 147/12 = 35/12 ≈ <b>2.92</b>, so σ ≈ 1.71. (A fair n-sided die has variance (n² − 1)/12.)' },
          `<p><b>What σ tells you about tails.</b> Whatever the distribution, <b>Chebyshev's inequality</b> says P(|X − μ| ≥ kσ) ≤ 1/k²: at most a quarter of the probability lies 2 or more standard deviations out, at most a ninth 3 or more. It follows from Markov's inequality applied to (X − μ)². For a normal distribution the true figures are far smaller (5% and 0.3%), so Chebyshev is a worst case. Real returns sit in between, which is why "a 5-sigma day" happens far more often than the normal distribution predicts.</p>`,
          { id: 'x1', q: 'Knowing only the mean and standard deviation, what is the largest possible probability of landing 3 or more standard deviations from the mean? (Give a decimal.)', a: 1 / 9, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'Chebyshev: 1/k².', sol: 'Chebyshev with k = 3: at most 1/9 ≈ <b>0.111</b>. For a normal distribution it\'s about 0.003; the bound holds for every distribution, so it has to allow for the worst.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Scaling and shifting', blocks: [
          `<p>Var(aX + b) = a² Var(X): shifting doesn't change spread, and scaling by a multiplies the standard deviation by |a|.</p>
           <p>Read it in units. If X is in dollars and you convert to cents, every distance from the mean grows 100-fold, so the variance (squared distances) grows 10,000-fold and σ grows 100-fold. Adding a constant fee moves every outcome and the mean together, so no distance changes.</p>
           <p>Combining the two gives <b>standardisation</b>: Z = (X − μ)/σ has mean 0 and variance 1. A z-score says how many standard deviations from the mean a value is, which lets you compare across different scales: a 3% move in a stock with 1% daily σ (z = 3) is far more unusual than a 3% move in one with 3% daily σ (z = 1).</p>`,
          { q: 'X is a die roll. What is Var(3X + 2)?', a: 9 * 35 / 12, tol: { abs: 0.01 }, hint: 'The +2 does nothing; the 3 gets squared.', sol: 'The +2 shifts every outcome equally, so it doesn\'t change spread. The ×3 triples every distance from the mean, so squared distances grow 9 times: 9 × 35/12 = <b>26.25</b>. The standard deviation triples, from 1.71 to 5.12.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Adding independent variables: the √n rule', blocks: [
          `<p>For independent X and Y, Var(X + Y) = Var(X) + Var(Y). So the total of n independent copies has standard deviation σ√n, and their average σ/√n. Averages get more precise slowly: four times the data halves the error.</p>
           <p>Note what adds: <b>variances</b>, not standard deviations. Two independent bets each with σ = $3 and $4 have combined σ = √(9 + 16) = $5, not $7. The errors partly cancel, because one is often up when the other is down.</p>
           <p>Indicators make this a quick tool. The number of heads in n flips is a sum of n independent indicators with variance p(1 − p) each, so Var = np(1 − p) and σ = √(np(1 − p)). For 100 fair flips: σ = √25 = 5 heads. So 60 heads is 2σ above the mean of 50: unusual, but not shocking.</p>`,
          { widget: 'sqrtn', predict: { q: 'Averaging 4 dice instead of 1 makes the spread of the average about…', opts: ['half as wide', 'a quarter as wide', 'the same', 'twice as wide'], then: 'Move the slider to 1, then 4, then 16.' } },
          { q: 'What is the standard deviation of the total of 100 dice?', a: Math.sqrt(100 * 35 / 12), tol: { abs: 0.05 }, hint: 'Add 100 variances, then take the square root.', sol: 'Independent variances add: 100 × 35/12 ≈ 291.7. Square root: √291.7 ≈ <b>17.1</b>. Against a mean total of 350, that\'s only about 5% of the total: sums of many independent pieces are relatively stable.' },
          { q: 'And of their average?', a: Math.sqrt(100 * 35 / 12) / 100, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'Divide the total by 100.', sol: 'The average is the total divided by 100, so its σ is 17.1/100 ≈ <b>0.171</b>. Equivalently σ/√n = 1.71/10. That\'s the law of large numbers in numbers: the average of 100 dice is within about ±0.34 (2σ) of 3.5 most of the time.' },
          { id: 'x2', q: 'A fair coin is flipped 400 times. What is the standard deviation of the number of heads?', a: 10, tol: { abs: 0.01 }, hint: '√(np(1 − p)).', sol: '√(400 × ½ × ½) = √100 = <b>10</b>. So 200 ± 20 heads covers about 95% of outcomes.' },
          { market: 'Volatility scales with √time. A stock with 1% daily volatility has about 1% × √252 ≈ 16% annual volatility, assuming independent days. That one line comes up in every quant interview.' },
        ] },
        { h: 'Covariance and correlation', blocks: [
          `<p>Cov(X, Y) = E[(X − μ<sub>X</sub>)(Y − μ<sub>Y</sub>)] = E[XY] − E[X]E[Y]. The product is positive when both are above their means or both below, so it measures how they move together. Correlation scales it to lie between −1 and 1:</p>
           <div class="hb-eq">ρ = Cov(X, Y)/(σ<sub>X</sub> σ<sub>Y</sub>)</div>`,
          { fig: 'plot', x: [-3, 3], y: [-3, 3], w: 300, h: 280, xt: [-2, 0, 2], yt: [-2, 0, 2], pts: Fig.scatter(70, 0.7, 11), xl: 'X', yl: 'Y', alt: 'A scatter of 70 points with correlation about 0.7, trending up and to the right.', cap: 'ρ ≈ 0.7. Most points fall in the top-right and bottom-left quadrants, where the product (X − μ)(Y − μ) is positive.' },
          `<p>In general Var(X ± Y) = Var(X) + Var(Y) ± 2Cov(X, Y). This is the maths of diversification: combining positively correlated bets reduces risk less.</p>
           <p>The rules for covariance mirror multiplication: Cov(aX, bY) = ab Cov(X, Y); Cov(X + Y, Z) = Cov(X, Z) + Cov(Y, Z); Cov(X, X) = Var(X); and independent variables have covariance 0. With those, the variance of any sum is "all the variances plus twice every pairwise covariance":</p>
           <div class="hb-eq">Var(Σ X<sub>i</sub>) = Σ Var(X<sub>i</sub>) + 2 Σ<sub>i&lt;j</sub> Cov(X<sub>i</sub>, X<sub>j</sub>)</div>`,
          { trap: 'Zero correlation doesn\'t mean independence. If X is symmetric about 0 and Y = X², Y is fixed by X, yet their correlation is 0. Correlation only sees straight-line relationships.' },
          { q: 'X and Y each have variance 1 and correlation 0.5. What is Var(X − Y)?', a: 1, hint: 'Var(X) + Var(Y) − 2Cov(X, Y), with Cov = ρσσ.', sol: 'Cov(X, Y) = ρσ<sub>X</sub>σ<sub>Y</sub> = 0.5 × 1 × 1 = 0.5. Var(X − Y) = 1 + 1 − 2 × 0.5 = <b>1</b>. Positive correlation makes a difference less variable: the moves partly cancel. (Var(X + Y) would be 3.)' },
          { q: 'X and Y are independent, each with variance 1. What is the correlation between X and X + Y?', a: Math.SQRT1_2, tol: { abs: 0.005 }, hint: 'Cov(X, X + Y) = Var(X). What is the standard deviation of X + Y?', sol: 'Cov(X, X + Y) = Cov(X, X) + Cov(X, Y) = 1 + 0 = 1. And sd(X + Y) = √(1 + 1) = √2. So ρ = 1/(1 × √2) ≈ <b>0.707</b>. This is the correlation of a stock with an index it makes up half of, if the other half is independent.' },
          { worked: 'variance of a count with dependent pieces', q: 'n people take hats at random. The expected number who get their own hat is 1. What is the variance?', steps: [
            ['Write the count as indicators I<sub>1</sub> + … + I<sub>n</sub>. What is Var(I<sub>i</sub>)?', 'p(1 − p) with p = 1/n: (1/n)(1 − 1/n).'],
            ['What is P(both i and j get their own hats)?', '(1/n) × 1/(n − 1): person i gets theirs, then j gets theirs from the n − 1 left.'],
            'So Cov(I<sub>i</sub>, I<sub>j</sub>) = 1/(n(n − 1)) − 1/n², slightly positive.',
            'Var = n × (1/n)(1 − 1/n) + n(n − 1) × [1/(n(n − 1)) − 1/n²] = (1 − 1/n) + (1 − (n − 1)/n) = <b>1</b>.',
            'Mean 1 and variance 1, for any n: the count behaves like a Poisson(1) variable, which it approaches as n grows.',
          ] },
        ] },
        { h: 'Two-asset portfolios', blocks: [
          `<p>Hold weights w₁ and w₂ in two assets. Its variance is the most used formula in portfolio risk:</p>
           <div class="hb-eq">σ<sub>p</sub>² = w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2w₁w₂ρσ₁σ₂</div>`,
          { worked: true, q: 'Two stocks each have 20% volatility and correlation 0.5. What is the volatility of a 50/50 portfolio?', steps: [
            ['Write down each term.', 'w₁²σ₁² = 0.25 × 0.04 = 0.01, and the same for the second stock.'],
            ['Now the cross term.', '2 × 0.5 × 0.5 × 0.5 × 0.2 × 0.2 = 0.01.'],
            ['Add and take the square root.', 'σ<sub>p</sub>² = 0.03, so σ<sub>p</sub> = √0.03 ≈ 17.3%.'],
            ['What would it be at ρ = 0 and ρ = 1?', 'ρ = 0: √0.02 ≈ 14.1%. ρ = 1: 20%, so no benefit at all.'],
          ] },
          `<p><b>The minimum-variance mix.</b> With w₁ = w and w₂ = 1 − w, set the derivative of σ<sub>p</sub>² to zero:</p>
           <div class="hb-eq">w* = (σ₂² − ρσ₁σ₂)/(σ₁² + σ₂² − 2ρσ₁σ₂)</div>
           <p>Sanity checks: with equal volatilities it gives ½ for any ρ < 1; with ρ = 0 it is σ₂²/(σ₁² + σ₂²), weighting each asset by the <i>other's</i> variance; with ρ = −1 the two can cancel exactly and σ<sub>p</sub> reaches 0.</p>
           <p><b>Many assets.</b> With n assets of equal volatility σ and equal pairwise correlation ρ, held equally, σ<sub>p</sub>² = σ²/n + (1 − 1/n)ρσ². The first term is stock-specific risk and dies away; the second is shared risk and stays. That floor, ρσ², is market risk.</p>`,
          { id: 'a1', q: 'n stocks are uncorrelated, each with 30% volatility. You hold them equally weighted, with n = 25. What is the portfolio\'s volatility, as a decimal?', a: 0.06, hint: 'σ/√n.', sol: 'Each stock has weight 1/25, so the variance is 25 × (1/25)² × 0.09 = 0.09/25 = 0.0036, with no covariance terms. σ = √0.0036 = <b>0.06</b>, or 6%: σ/√n = 30%/5.' },
          { id: 'm1', q: '100 stocks all have 30% volatility and pairwise correlation 0.25. You hold them equally. As you add more stocks, the portfolio volatility…', opts: [['falls to about 15%'], ['falls towards 0, given enough names', 'That needs zero correlation.'], ['stays at 30%, as all stocks are risky', 'Diversification helps while ρ is below 1.'], ['falls to 3%', 'That\'s 30%/√100, which ignores correlation.']], sol: 'For equal weights, variance = σ²/n + (1 − 1/n)ρσ² → ρσ². So volatility → √0.25 × 30% = 15%. With 100 names you\'re already at √(0.0009 + 0.99 × 0.0225) ≈ 15.2%. Common factors put a floor under risk, which is why market-neutral hedging matters.' },
          { id: 'x3', q: 'Two assets have volatilities 10% and 20% and correlation 0.5. What weight in the first asset gives the minimum-variance portfolio (weights sum to 1)?', a: 1, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'w* = (σ₂² − ρσ₁σ₂)/(σ₁² + σ₂² − 2ρσ₁σ₂).', sol: 'Numerator: 0.04 − 0.5 × 0.1 × 0.2 = 0.03. Denominator: 0.01 + 0.04 − 2 × 0.01 = 0.03. So w* = <b>1</b>: hold only the low-volatility asset. With this much correlation, the riskier asset adds more risk than it diversifies, at any positive weight.' },
        ] },
      ],
      takeaways: ['Var = E[X²] − (E[X])²; σ = √Var. A 0–1 variable has variance p(1 − p).', 'Chebyshev: at most 1/k² of the probability lies k or more σ from the mean, for any distribution.', 'Var(aX + b) = a² Var(X). z-scores put different scales on one footing.', 'Independent variances add (not standard deviations): totals spread like √n, averages shrink like 1/√n.', 'Var of a sum = sum of variances + 2 × every pairwise covariance.', 'ρ only sees straight lines: zero correlation ≠ independence.', 'Portfolio variance: w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2w₁w₂ρσ₁σ₂, and shared risk ρσ² never diversifies away.'],
      explain: { q: 'Why does the risk of a portfolio stop falling, however many stocks you add?', a: 'The stock-specific part of each stock\'s risk averages away as you add names, like σ²/n. The part they share, the covariance, is in every pair and doesn\'t average away. So variance falls to the average covariance, and no further. That remaining market risk is what hedging with an index or futures removes.' },
      homework: [
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'X takes the values 0 and 1 with P(1) = p = 0.3. What is its variance?', a: 0.21, hint: 'p(1 − p).', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> E[X²] − (E[X])², using X² = X for a 0–1 variable.</p><ol><li>E[X] = 0.3, and E[X²] = E[X] = 0.3.</li><li>Var = 0.3 − 0.09 = <b>0.21</b>, which is p(1 − p) = 0.3 × 0.7.</li></ol><p><b>Check:</b> p(1 − p) peaks at 0.25 when p = ½, so 0.21 is in range. σ = √0.21 ≈ 0.46.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'Daily volatility is 2%. What is the volatility over 25 days, as a decimal, assuming independent days?', a: 0.1, hint: '√25.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> variances of independent days add, so volatility grows with √time.</p><ol><li>Daily variance: 0.02² = 0.0004. Over 25 days: 25 × 0.0004 = 0.01.</li><li>Volatility: √0.01 = <b>0.10</b>, i.e. 2% × √25 = 10%.</li></ol><p><b>The trap:</b> 25 × 2% = 50% adds standard deviations, which assumes every day moves the same way. Independent days partly cancel.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'Var(X) = 4, Var(Y) = 9, Cov(X, Y) = 3. What is the correlation?', a: 0.5, hint: 'Cov/(σσ).', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> divide the covariance by both standard deviations.</p><ol><li>σ<sub>X</sub> = √4 = 2, σ<sub>Y</sub> = √9 = 3.</li><li>ρ = 3/(2 × 3) = <b>0.5</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Check:</b> the result must be between −1 and 1. A covariance of 3 is half the largest possible (6), so ρ = 0.5.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'With those numbers, what is Var(2X − Y)?', a: 13, hint: '4Var X + Var Y − 4Cov.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> Var(aX + bY) = a²Var X + b²Var Y + 2ab Cov(X, Y), with a = 2, b = −1.</p><ol><li>a²Var X = 4 × 4 = 16.</li><li>b²Var Y = 1 × 9 = 9.</li><li>2ab Cov = 2 × 2 × (−1) × 3 = −12.</li><li>Total: 16 + 9 − 12 = <b>13</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Sense check:</b> X and Y move together, so subtracting Y cancels some of 2X\'s movement: the result is below 16 + 9 = 25.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'core', q: 'Annual volatility is 16%. What is the rough daily volatility, in percent? (Use √252 ≈ 16.)', a: 1, tol: { abs: 0.03 }, hint: 'Divide by 16.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> annual σ = daily σ × √252, so divide by √252 ≈ 16.</p><ol><li>16%/16 = <b>1%</b> a day.</li></ol><p><b>Rule of thumb worth memorising:</b> divide annual volatility by 16 to get a typical daily move. A stock with 32% volatility moves about 2% on a normal day.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'Two assets have 10% and 20% volatility and correlation −1. What weight in the first asset gives a zero-risk portfolio (weights sum to 1)?', a: 2 / 3, tol: { abs: 0.002 }, hint: 'With ρ = −1, σ<sub>p</sub> = |w₁σ₁ − w₂σ₂|.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> with ρ = −1 the variance is a perfect square, (w₁σ₁ − w₂σ₂)², so it can be made zero.</p><ol><li>Set w × 0.1 = (1 − w) × 0.2.</li><li>0.1w = 0.2 − 0.2w, so 0.3w = 0.2 and w = <b>2/3</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Interpretation:</b> hold twice as much of the calmer asset, so its moves exactly offset the other\'s. The formula w* = (σ₂² − ρσ₁σ₂)/(σ₁² + σ₂² − 2ρσ₁σ₂) gives (0.04 + 0.02)/(0.01 + 0.04 + 0.04) = 0.06/0.09 = 2/3 too.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'interview', q: 'Assets have 20% and 10% volatility and correlation 0. What weight in the first minimises variance (weights sum to 1)?', a: 0.2, hint: 'Minimum-variance weight is σ₂²/(σ₁² + σ₂²) when ρ = 0.', sol: '<p><b>Approach:</b> minimise w²σ₁² + (1 − w)²σ₂².</p><ol><li>Differentiate and set to zero: 2wσ₁² − 2(1 − w)σ₂² = 0, so w = σ₂²/(σ₁² + σ₂²).</li><li>w = 0.01/(0.04 + 0.01) = <b>0.2</b>.</li><li>The portfolio variance is then 0.04 × 0.04 + 0.64 × 0.01 = 0.008, a volatility of about 8.9%, below either asset\'s.</li></ol><p><b>Point:</b> with zero correlation even a risky asset belongs in the minimum-risk mix, in proportion to the other\'s variance.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'stretch', q: 'Prove that correlation always lies between −1 and 1. (Hint: consider Var(X/σ<sub>X</sub> ± Y/σ<sub>Y</sub>).)', sol: '<p><b>Proof:</b> variance can\'t be negative, so use it on standardised variables.</p><ol><li>Var(X/σ<sub>X</sub> + Y/σ<sub>Y</sub>) = Var X/σ<sub>X</sub>² + Var Y/σ<sub>Y</sub>² + 2Cov(X, Y)/(σ<sub>X</sub>σ<sub>Y</sub>) = 1 + 1 + 2ρ = 2 + 2ρ ≥ 0, so ρ ≥ −1.</li><li>Var(X/σ<sub>X</sub> − Y/σ<sub>Y</sub>) = 2 − 2ρ ≥ 0, so ρ ≤ 1.</li></ol><p><b>Bonus:</b> ρ = 1 exactly when the second variance is 0, i.e. X/σ<sub>X</sub> − Y/σ<sub>Y</sub> is constant: Y is an increasing straight-line function of X. That\'s why correlation measures linear relationships.</p>' },
        { lvl: 'lab', q: 'Take any 20 consecutive daily closing prices of a stock. Compute the daily returns, their standard deviation, and annualise it with √252. How does it compare with what a volatility page says for that stock?', sol: '<p>You\'ll usually be in the right area but noisy. With n returns, a standard-deviation estimate has a relative standard error of about 1/√(2n); for 19 returns that\'s about 16% of the estimate. So a true 25% volatility often shows up anywhere from 17% to 33%.</p><p><b>Why it matters:</b> volatility estimates from short windows wobble a lot, and real volatility changes over time too. Risk systems weigh these two effects when choosing a window (or use exponentially weighted estimates).</p>' },
      ],
      practice: [['Moments topic', '#/topic/moments'], ['Formula cards', '#/flashcards'], ['Portfolio question', '#/sse/all/any/portfolio']],
    },
  });
})();
